{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N6TPW4V4HCd5JxiK7mZuT4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — gen-depth-fingerprint\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: AAAI 2027 (deadline: August 2, 2026)\n5\t- Stretch: EMNLP 2026 ARR (deadline: May 25, 2026) — minimal scope RQ1+RQ4\n6\t- Fallback venues: ICLR 2027 (Sep 24), NAACL 2027 ARR (Oct 16)\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\tNon-paraphrastic LLM generation chains exhibit **dual depth dynamics** that cross-cuts the surface/reference distinction: **level/diversity features** (entropy_mean, TTR, hapax ratio) drift monotonically with depth (H₂), while **distributional shape features** (entropy_std, entropy_max, tail_decay) remain near-stationary (H₁). Notably, entropy_mean is a reference-model feature that drifts (ρ=-0.68), showing the H₁/H₂ split is about what the feature measures (central tendency vs shape), not where it comes from (surface vs reference). This dual pattern creates a complementary feature space: drifting features provide ordinal depth signal, while stable shape features provide noise-robust class boundaries. The combination significantly outperforms either alone (macro-F1 0.82 vs 0.76/0.72).\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条，均可证伪)\n12\t\n13\t**Claim 1 — Depth Discriminability**: Distributional features computed under a held-out reference model can classify text generation depth (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) with macro-F1 significantly above chance, generalizing across unseen generators (leave-one-generator-out evaluation).\n14\t\n15\t**Claim 2 — Dual Dynamics (Level vs Shape)**: Depth-dependent features split into two families by **what they measure**, not by their source (surface vs reference): (a) **level/diversity features** (entropy_mean, TTR, hapax_ratio) drift monotonically with depth (H₂ — vocabulary impoverishment + entropy compression); (b) **shape features** (entropy_std, entropy_max, tail_decay) remain near-stationary (H₁ — distributional shape is robust to recursive generation). This split cross-cuts the surface/reference boundary (entropy_mean is reference-derived but drifts). Full feature set (0.82) significantly outperforms surface-only (0.76) and reference-only (0.72) — the two dynamics are complementary for classification.\n16\t\n17\t**Claim 3 — Downstream Utility**: Predicted generation depth correlates with fine-tuning quality degradation (Spearman ρ), and depth-aware filtering outperforms binary detection baselines (FastDetectGPT, LD-Score threshold) for preserving downstream model quality.\n18\t\n19\t## Story Spine\n20\t\n21\t**Problem**: Web-scraped training data increasingly contains LLM-generated text of unknown provenance depth. Current detection methods offer only binary real-vs-synthetic classification, forcing all-or-nothing filtering: discard everything flagged as synthetic (losing useful gen-1 content) or keep everything (admitting high-risk deep-generation text with compounding distributional drift).\n22\t\n23\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Binary detectors (FastDetectGPT, SynthID-Text, LD-Score) cannot distinguish gen-1 from gen-2+. Attractor cycle theory (ACL 2025) proves paraphrasing chains converge to 2-period cycles, bounding depth signal to ~3 classes — but this result depends on the semantic-preservation constraint of paraphrasing. No existing work attempts generation depth estimation.\n24\t\n25\t**Our framing**: We characterize distributional drift in recursive non-paraphrastic LLM generation, revealing **dual dynamics**: level/diversity features (including reference-model entropy_mean) drift monotonically with depth (H₂), while distributional shape features (entropy variance, tail decay) remain near-stationary (H₁). This split is about **what the feature measures** (central tendency vs shape), not where it comes from — overturning the naive expectation that \"reference-model features = robust, surface features = fragile.\" The dual pattern has practical value: drifting features provide ordinal depth signal, stable shape features provide noise-robust decision boundaries, and their combination yields the best depth estimation for risk-stratified data curation.\n26\t\n27\t**Headline result template**: \"On multi-generator continuation chains across 4 domains, our distributional fingerprint features achieve macro-F1 of X for 3-class depth estimation (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+), with leave-one-generator-out generalization at Y. Depth-aware filtering preserves Z% more downstream task performance compared to binary filtering baselines.\"\n28\t\n29\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n30\t\n31\t**RC1 — \"Is this just vocabulary impoverishment?\"**: Surface features alone achieve 0.76 macro-F1, but full features achieve 0.82 (p<0.001). We don't claim reference features are \"better\" — we show they capture a **different, complementary** signal (stationary distributional shape vs drifting level/diversity). The dual dynamics finding is itself a scientific contribution. Note: \"fingerprint\" in the paper refers to the full feature vector's combination, not just reference-model features.\n32\t\n33\t**RC7 — Pre-registered prediction (LD-Score)**: LD-Score (character-distribution signature) measures character-level distributional properties. We predict it is a **level feature** (sensitive to vocabulary/character frequency shifts) and should exhibit **H₂ drift** with depth. This prediction is registered before plan_011 execution as an out-of-sample test of the level/shape framework.\n34\t\n35\t**RC2 — \"Does this generalize beyond Pythia?\"**: MVP used same-family models (Pythia-2.8B generator + Pythia-410M reference). → Cross-family experiments: Qwen/OLMo generators + non-Pythia reference models (RQ1 cross-generator evaluation).\n36\t\n37\t**RC3 — \"Is 3-class sufficient? Why not finer-grained?\"**: Attractor theory bounds paraphrasing to ~3 classes. → Secondary analysis: finer-grained ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on non-paraphrastic chains to test if more depth signal exists.\n38\t\n39\t**RC4 — \"How realistic are single-model chains?\"**: Real web contamination involves diverse generators. → Cross-model chains (different generator per depth step) as primary evaluation setting (RQ3).\n40\t\n41\t**RC5 — \"What about human editing / post-processing?\"**: Real-world synthetic text is often edited. → Robustness test with simulated human edits (random word replacement as lower-bound + LLM-simulated editing).\n42\t\n43\t**RC6 — \"Ecological validity — continuation vs real web content\"**: Lab-constructed chains may not match wild distribution. → Multi-domain seeds (news, Wikipedia, scientific, creative) + multiple prompt types (continuation, elaboration, inspired rewriting).\n44\t\n45\t**RC8 — \"Aren't these just OOD detection features?\"**: Reference-model entropy statistics are functionally NLL-based OOD scores (same signal backbone as DetectGPT/GLTR/Binoculars). Our contribution is NOT the features themselves but: (a) the problem formulation (ordinal depth estimation, not binary detection), (b) the empirical discovery that these features exhibit dual dynamics (level drift vs shape stationarity) under recursive generation, and (c) the practical application to risk-stratified data curation. Paper must explicitly connect to OOD detection + perplexity-based detection literature and position the contribution as primarily empirical/conceptual rather than methodological.\n46\t\n47\t**RC9 — \"Only ≤2.8B generators — does this transfer to frontier scale?\"**: All 5 current generators ≤2.8B. Frontier models (7B+) produce more fluent text that may compress depth signal. → Add at least one 7B+ generator (plan_014). If depth signal significantly weaker at scale → honest limitation with analysis of why; if comparable → strong generalization evidence.\n48\t\n49\t**RC10 — \"Text length confound\"**: Hapax ratio and TTR are mechanically length-dependent. If generation chains produce texts of systematically different lengths, surface features may track length artifacts rather than distributional drift. → Length distribution analysis + length-normalized features + length-matched comparison (plan_013). Critical confound control.\n50\t\n51\t**RC11 — \"Downstream utility — single run, no CIs\"**: Depth-aware filtering advantage must be validated with multiple seeds + bootstrap CIs + sample-count-matched comparison (to control for data size confound). Must use non-Pythia model for downstream fine-tuning (OLMo-1B) to avoid reference model circularity (Pythia-410M as both feature extractor and eval model).\n52\t\n53\t## Contribution Positioning (Dream D006 insight)\n54\tThe contribution is **primarily empirical and conceptual**, not methodological. The features (NLL summary statistics) and classifier (XGBoost) are standard — the novelty lies in:\n55\t1. **Problem formulation**: Ordinal depth estimation as a new task beyond binary detection\n56\t2. **Empirical discovery**: Dual dynamics (level/diversity drift vs shape stationarity) cross-cutting surface/reference boundary\n57\t3. **Practical application**: Risk-stratified data curation outperforming binary filtering\n58\t\n59\tDo NOT overclaim \"distributional fingerprinting\" as a new method. Position the paper as empirical science with a novel task formulation and a surprising empirical finding. The H1/H2 framework is an empirical validation extending attractor cycle theory to non-paraphrastic chains, not a major theoretical contribution.\n60\t\n61\tRelated work must expand to 30+ references covering: perplexity-based detection (DetectGPT, GLTR, DNA-GPT, Binoculars), model collapse theory (Alemohammad et al., Dohmatob et al., Gerstgrasser et al., Guo et al.), OOD detection (Hendrycks & Gimpel), data quality scoring (DSIR, QuRating, CCNet), watermarking (Kirchenbauer et al.), vocabulary diversity (Guo et al. 2024, Padmakumar & He 2024, Holtzman et al. 2020).\n62\t\n63\t## Venue Fit\n64\t- AAAI 2027: Fit as empirical + conceptual contribution — novel task formulation (depth estimation) + surprising empirical finding (dual dynamics) + practical application (data curation). Must position as empirical science, not methodology paper.\n65\t- EMNLP 2026: Excellent fit — directly addresses special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (data contamination, synthetic data feedback loops, data governance). NLP-native venue for text-focused work. Empirical/conceptual contributions better received here than AAAI.\n66\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f50109c-746c-4b8f-986a-349fb461f53e","uuid":"94bff9be-2399-4198-9b31-c9952e3aa63f","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:37:05.728Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T221538/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — gen-depth-fingerprint\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: AAAI 2027 (deadline: August 2, 2026)\n- Stretch: EMNLP 2026 ARR (deadline: May 25, 2026) — minimal scope RQ1+RQ4\n- Fallback venues: ICLR 2027 (Sep 24), NAACL 2027 ARR (Oct 16)\n\n## Core Insight\nNon-paraphrastic LLM generation chains exhibit **dual depth dynamics** that cross-cuts the surface/reference distinction: **level/diversity features** (entropy_mean, TTR, hapax ratio) drift monotonically with depth (H₂), while **distributional shape features** (entropy_std, entropy_max, tail_decay) remain near-stationary (H₁). Notably, entropy_mean is a reference-model feature that drifts (ρ=-0.68), showing the H₁/H₂ split is about what the feature measures (central tendency vs shape), not where it comes from (surface vs reference). This dual pattern creates a complementary feature space: drifting features provide ordinal depth signal, while stable shape features provide noise-robust class boundaries. The combination significantly outperforms either alone (macro-F1 0.82 vs 0.76/0.72).\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，均可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 — Depth Discriminability**: Distributional features computed under a held-out reference model can classify text generation depth (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) with macro-F1 significantly above chance, generalizing across unseen generators (leave-one-generator-out evaluation).\n\n**Claim 2 — Dual Dynamics (Level vs Shape)**: Depth-dependent features split into two families by **what they measure**, not by their source (surface vs reference): (a) **level/diversity features** (entropy_mean, TTR, hapax_ratio) drift monotonically with depth (H₂ — vocabulary impoverishment + entropy compression); (b) **shape features** (entropy_std, entropy_max, tail_decay) remain near-stationary (H₁ — distributional shape is robust to recursive generation). This split cross-cuts the surface/reference boundary (entropy_mean is reference-derived but drifts). Full feature set (0.82) significantly outperforms surface-only (0.76) and reference-only (0.72) — the two dynamics are complementary for classification.\n\n**Claim 3 — Downstream Utility**: Predicted generation depth correlates with fine-tuning quality degradation (Spearman ρ), and depth-aware filtering outperforms binary detection baselines (FastDetectGPT, LD-Score threshold) for preserving downstream model quality.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: Web-scraped training data increasingly contains LLM-generated text of unknown provenance depth. Current detection methods offer only binary real-vs-synthetic classification, forcing all-or-nothing filtering: discard everything flagged as synthetic (losing useful gen-1 content) or keep everything (admitting high-risk deep-generation text with compounding distributional drift).\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Binary detectors (FastDetectGPT, SynthID-Text, LD-Score) cannot distinguish gen-1 from gen-2+. Attractor cycle theory (ACL 2025) proves paraphrasing chains converge to 2-period cycles, bounding depth signal to ~3 classes — but this result depends on the semantic-preservation constraint of paraphrasing. No existing work attempts generation depth estimation.\n\n**Our framing**: We characterize distributional drift in recursive non-paraphrastic LLM generation, revealing **dual dynamics**: level/diversity features (including reference-model entropy_mean) drift monotonically with depth (H₂), while distributional shape features (entropy variance, tail decay) remain near-stationary (H₁). This split is about **what the feature measures** (central tendency vs shape), not where it comes from — overturning the naive expectation that \"reference-model features = robust, surface features = fragile.\" The dual pattern has practical value: drifting features provide ordinal depth signal, stable shape features provide noise-robust decision boundaries, and their combination yields the best depth estimation for risk-stratified data curation.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"On multi-generator continuation chains across 4 domains, our distributional fingerprint features achieve macro-F1 of X for 3-class depth estimation (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+), with leave-one-generator-out generalization at Y. Depth-aware filtering preserves Z% more downstream task performance compared to binary filtering baselines.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n**RC1 — \"Is this just vocabulary impoverishment?\"**: Surface features alone achieve 0.76 macro-F1, but full features achieve 0.82 (p<0.001). We don't claim reference features are \"better\" — we show they capture a **different, complementary** signal (stationary distributional shape vs drifting level/diversity). The dual dynamics finding is itself a scientific contribution. Note: \"fingerprint\" in the paper refers to the full feature vector's combination, not just reference-model features.\n\n**RC7 — Pre-registered prediction (LD-Score)**: LD-Score (character-distribution signature) measures character-level distributional properties. We predict it is a **level feature** (sensitive to vocabulary/character frequency shifts) and should exhibit **H₂ drift** with depth. This prediction is registered before plan_011 execution as an out-of-sample test of the level/shape framework.\n\n**RC2 — \"Does this generalize beyond Pythia?\"**: MVP used same-family models (Pythia-2.8B generator + Pythia-410M reference). → Cross-family experiments: Qwen/OLMo generators + non-Pythia reference models (RQ1 cross-generator evaluation).\n\n**RC3 — \"Is 3-class sufficient? Why not finer-grained?\"**: Attractor theory bounds paraphrasing to ~3 classes. → Secondary analysis: finer-grained ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on non-paraphrastic chains to test if more depth signal exists.\n\n**RC4 — \"How realistic are single-model chains?\"**: Real web contamination involves diverse generators. → Cross-model chains (different generator per depth step) as primary evaluation setting (RQ3).\n\n**RC5 — \"What about human editing / post-processing?\"**: Real-world synthetic text is often edited. → Robustness test with simulated human edits (random word replacement as lower-bound + LLM-simulated editing).\n\n**RC6 — \"Ecological validity — continuation vs real web content\"**: Lab-constructed chains may not match wild distribution. → Multi-domain seeds (news, Wikipedia, scientific, creative) + multiple prompt types (continuation, elaboration, inspired rewriting).\n\n**RC8 — \"Aren't these just OOD detection features?\"**: Reference-model entropy statistics are functionally NLL-based OOD scores (same signal backbone as DetectGPT/GLTR/Binoculars). Our contribution is NOT the features themselves but: (a) the problem formulation (ordinal depth estimation, not binary detection), (b) the empirical discovery that these features exhibit dual dynamics (level drift vs shape stationarity) under recursive generation, and (c) the practical application to risk-stratified data curation. Paper must explicitly connect to OOD detection + perplexity-based detection literature and position the contribution as primarily empirical/conceptual rather than methodological.\n\n**RC9 — \"Only ≤2.8B generators — does this transfer to frontier scale?\"**: All 5 current generators ≤2.8B. Frontier models (7B+) produce more fluent text that may compress depth signal. → Add at least one 7B+ generator (plan_014). If depth signal significantly weaker at scale → honest limitation with analysis of why; if comparable → strong generalization evidence.\n\n**RC10 — \"Text length confound\"**: Hapax ratio and TTR are mechanically length-dependent. If generation chains produce texts of systematically different lengths, surface features may track length artifacts rather than distributional drift. → Length distribution analysis + length-normalized features + length-matched comparison (plan_013). Critical confound control.\n\n**RC11 — \"Downstream utility — single run, no CIs\"**: Depth-aware filtering advantage must be validated with multiple seeds + bootstrap CIs + sample-count-matched comparison (to control for data size confound). Must use non-Pythia model for downstream fine-tuning (OLMo-1B) to avoid reference model circularity (Pythia-410M as both feature extractor and eval model).\n\n## Contribution Positioning (Dream D006 insight)\nThe contribution is **primarily empirical and conceptual**, not methodological. The features (NLL summary statistics) and classifier (XGBoost) are standard — the novelty lies in:\n1. **Problem formulation**: Ordinal depth estimation as a new task beyond binary detection\n2. **Empirical discovery**: Dual dynamics (level/diversity drift vs shape stationarity) cross-cutting surface/reference boundary\n3. **Practical application**: Risk-stratified data curation outperforming binary filtering\n\nDo NOT overclaim \"distributional fingerprinting\" as a new method. Position the paper as empirical science with a novel task formulation and a surprising empirical finding. The H1/H2 framework is an empirical validation extending attractor cycle theory to non-paraphrastic chains, not a major theoretical contribution.\n\nRelated work must expand to 30+ references covering: perplexity-based detection (DetectGPT, GLTR, DNA-GPT, Binoculars), model collapse theory (Alemohammad et al., Dohmatob et al., Gerstgrasser et al., Guo et al.), OOD detection (Hendrycks & Gimpel), data quality scoring (DSIR, QuRating, CCNet), watermarking (Kirchenbauer et al.), vocabulary diversity (Guo et al. 2024, Padmakumar & He 2024, Holtzman et al. 2020).\n\n## Venue Fit\n- AAAI 2027: Fit as empirical + conceptual contribution — novel task formulation (depth estimation) + surprising empirical finding (dual dynamics) + practical application (data curation). Must position as empirical science, not methodology paper.\n- EMNLP 2026: Excellent fit — directly addresses special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (data contamination, synthetic data feedback loops, data governance). NLP-native venue for text-focused work. Empirical/conceptual contributions better received here than AAAI.\n","numLines":66,"startLine":1,"totalLines":66}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CyhCdxnDhGpCumbc1CWUmh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"feature_ablation_and_trend\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Three-part analysis on MVP data (4609 records, Pythia-2.8B generator, Pythia-410M reference). (A) Feature ablation: reference-only vs surface-only vs full. (B) H1/H2 trend analysis per feature. (C) Perplexity pre-validation for Claim 3.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"full_macro_f1\": 0.8204,\n7\t      \"surface_only_f1\": 0.7628,\n8\t      \"reference_only_f1\": 0.7187,\n9\t      \"full_vs_surface_f1_gap\": 0.058,\n10\t      \"full_vs_reference_f1_gap\": 0.102,\n11\t      \"ref_vs_surf_bootstrap_ci\": \"[0.706,0.733] vs [0.747,0.774]\",\n12\t      \"h2_features\": \"entropy_mean, ttr, hapax_ratio\",\n13\t      \"h1_features\": \"tail_decay, entropy_std, entropy_max, entropy_min\",\n14\t      \"entropy_mean_slope_r2\": 0.91,\n15\t      \"ttr_slope_r2\": 0.88,\n16\t      \"hapax_ratio_slope_r2\": 0.85,\n17\t      \"entropy_std_slope_r2\": 0.12,\n18\t      \"tail_decay_slope_r2\": 0.09,\n19\t      \"median_ppl_by_depth\": [\n20\t        13.5,\n21\t        4.96,\n22\t        3.0,\n23\t        2.07,\n24\t        1.72\n25\t      ],\n26\t      \"ppl_depth_spearman\": -0.97\n27\t    },\n28\t    \"tables\": [\n29\t      {\n30\t        \"name\": \"Table 1: Feature Ablation Results (3-class)\",\n31\t        \"columns\": [\n32\t          \"Feature Set\",\n33\t          \"Features\",\n34\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n35\t          \"gen-0 F1\",\n36\t          \"gen-1 F1\",\n37\t          \"gen-2+ F1\",\n38\t          \"CV std\"\n39\t        ],\n40\t        \"rows\": [\n41\t          [\n42\t            \"Reference-only\",\n43\t            \"entropy_{mean,std,min,max}, tail_decay\",\n44\t            0.72,\n45\t            0.88,\n46\t            0.63,\n47\t            0.65,\n48\t            0.021\n49\t          ],\n50\t          [\n51\t            \"Surface-only\",\n52\t            \"ttr, hapax_ratio\",\n53\t            0.76,\n54\t            0.9,\n55\t            0.69,\n56\t            0.7,\n57\t            0.019\n58\t          ],\n59\t          [\n60\t            \"Full\",\n61\t            \"All 7 features\",\n62\t            0.82,\n63\t            0.95,\n64\t            0.76,\n65\t            0.79,\n66\t            0.013\n67\t          ]\n68\t        ]\n69\t      },\n70\t      {\n71\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: H1/H2 Feature Trend Analysis (linear regression slope per depth)\",\n72\t        \"columns\": [\n73\t          \"Feature\",\n74\t          \"Slope\",\n75\t          \"R²\",\n76\t          \"Category\",\n77\t          \"Interpretation\"\n78\t        ],\n79\t        \"rows\": [\n80\t          [\n81\t            \"entropy_mean\",\n82\t            -0.18,\n83\t            0.91,\n84\t            \"H2-drift\",\n85\t            \"Reference-model surprise decreases monotonically\"\n86\t          ],\n87\t          [\n88\t            \"ttr\",\n89\t            -0.024,\n90\t            0.88,\n91\t            \"H2-drift\",\n92\t            \"Lexical diversity declines with depth\"\n93\t          ],\n94\t          [\n95\t            \"hapax_ratio\",\n96\t            -0.019,\n97\t            0.85,\n98\t            \"H2-drift\",\n99\t            \"Singleton word ratio drops\"\n100\t          ],\n101\t          [\n102\t            \"entropy_std\",\n103\t            0.003,\n104\t            0.12,\n105\t            \"H1-stationary\",\n106\t            \"Surprise variance near-constant\"\n107\t          ],\n108\t          [\n109\t            \"entropy_max\",\n110\t            0.008,\n111\t            0.15,\n112\t            \"H1-stationary\",\n113\t            \"Tail surprise stable\"\n114\t          ],\n115\t          [\n116\t            \"entropy_min\",\n117\t            -0.005,\n118\t            0.11,\n119\t            \"H1-stationary\",\n120\t            \"Floor surprise stable\"\n121\t          ],\n122\t          [\n123\t            \"tail_decay\",\n124\t            0.002,\n125\t            0.09,\n126\t            \"H1-stationary\",\n127\t            \"Distributional shape robust\"\n128\t          ]\n129\t        ]\n130\t      }\n131\t    ],\n132\t    \"figures\": [],\n133\t    \"analysis\": \"The ablation gap is central to the paper's contribution positioning. The full_vs_surface gap (0.058) and full_vs_reference gap (0.102) confirm that both feature families carry non-redundant signal. Critically, entropy_mean is reference-derived but drifts (H2) — this cross-cuts the surface/reference boundary and is the headline finding. The H1/H2 split is clean: R²≥0.85 for drift features, R²≤0.15 for stationary features. This is the empirical backbone of Claim 2.\\n\\n\\n[ACTUAL — plan-001-fix completed] Full > surface > reference ablation gap confirmed. Surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) are stronger than initially expected (0.76 vs prior dream's 0.63) because vocabulary impoverishment is a robust, high-signal depth cue. Reference features alone achieve 0.72 — weaker than surface alone, overturning the naive expectation that reference features dominate. But their complementary value is clear: full set (0.82) significantly exceeds both subsets. The key insight is that entropy_mean (reference-derived) is an H2/drift feature while entropy_std/max/min (also reference-derived) are H1/stationary — the split is about what's measured (level vs shape), not the feature source. Perplexity monotonically decreases with depth (13.5→1.7) confirming that deeper text is progressively more predictable under the reference model — strong pre-validation for Claim 3's downstream utility hypothesis.\\n\"\n134\t  },\n135\t  {\n136\t    \"name\": \"cross_generator_evaluation\",\n137\t    \"description\": \"5 generators: Pythia-2.8B, Pythia-1B, OLMo-2-0425-1B, Qwen3-1.7B-Base, Qwen3-0.6B-Base. 1000 seeds × 5 generators × 5 depths = 25K chains. Leave-one-generator-out (LOGO) CV. Pythia-410M as reference model for all.\\n\",\n138\t    \"results\": {\n139\t      \"logo_macro_f1_mean\": 0.74,\n140\t      \"logo_macro_f1_std\": 0.03,\n141\t      \"logo_gen0_f1_mean\": 0.87,\n142\t      \"logo_gen1_f1_mean\": 0.68,\n143\t      \"logo_gen2plus_f1_mean\": 0.7,\n144\t      \"in_distribution_pooled_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n145\t      \"pythia_2_8b_held_out_f1\": 0.77,\n146\t      \"pythia_1b_held_out_f1\": 0.76,\n147\t      \"olmo2_held_out_f1\": 0.73,\n148\t      \"qwen3_1_7b_held_out_f1\": 0.72,\n149\t      \"qwen3_0_6b_held_out_f1\": 0.7,\n150\t      \"leave_one_family_out_pythia_f1\": 0.71,\n151\t      \"leave_one_family_out_qwen_f1\": 0.74,\n152\t      \"total_chains\": 23800,\n153\t      \"attrition_rate_pct\": 4.8\n154\t    },\n155\t    \"tables\": [\n156\t      {\n157\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Leave-One-Generator-Out Cross-Validation\",\n158\t        \"columns\": [\n159\t          \"Held-out Generator\",\n160\t          \"Family\",\n161\t          \"Params\",\n162\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n163\t          \"gen-0 F1\",\n164\t          \"gen-1 F1\",\n165\t          \"gen-2+ F1\"\n166\t        ],\n167\t        \"rows\": [\n168\t          [\n169\t            \"Pythia-2.8B\",\n170\t            \"Pythia\",\n171\t            \"2.8B\",\n172\t            0.77,\n173\t            0.9,\n174\t            0.71,\n175\t            0.72\n176\t          ],\n177\t          [\n178\t            \"Pythia-1B\",\n179\t            \"Pythia\",\n180\t            \"1B\",\n181\t            0.76,\n182\t            0.89,\n183\t            0.7,\n184\t            0.71\n185\t          ],\n186\t          [\n187\t            \"OLMo-2-0425-1B\",\n188\t            \"OLMo\",\n189\t            \"1.5B\",\n190\t            0.73,\n191\t            0.86,\n192\t            0.67,\n193\t            0.68\n194\t          ],\n195\t          [\n196\t            \"Qwen3-1.7B-Base\",\n197\t            \"Qwen\",\n198\t            \"1.7B\",\n199\t            0.72,\n200\t            0.85,\n201\t            0.66,\n202\t            0.67\n203\t          ],\n204\t          [\n205\t            \"Qwen3-0.6B-Base\",\n206\t            \"Qwen\",\n207\t            \"0.6B\",\n208\t            0.7,\n209\t            0.83,\n210\t            0.63,\n211\t            0.65\n212\t          ],\n213\t          [\n214\t            \"Mean ± std\",\n215\t            \"—\",\n216\t            \"—\",\n217\t            \"0.74 ± 0.03\",\n218\t            \"0.87 ± 0.03\",\n219\t            \"0.68 ± 0.03\",\n220\t            \"0.70 ± 0.03\"\n221\t          ]\n222\t        ]\n223\t      },\n224\t      {\n225\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Per-Generator In-Distribution 5-Fold CV\",\n226\t        \"columns\": [\n227\t          \"Generator\",\n228\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n229\t          \"CV std\"\n230\t        ],\n231\t        \"rows\": [\n232\t          [\n233\t            \"Pythia-2.8B\",\n234\t            0.84,\n235\t            0.014\n236\t          ],\n237\t          [\n238\t            \"Pythia-1B\",\n239\t            0.82,\n240\t            0.016\n241\t          ],\n242\t          [\n243\t            \"OLMo-2-0425-1B\",\n244\t            0.83,\n245\t            0.015\n246\t          ],\n247\t          [\n248\t            \"Qwen3-1.7B-Base\",\n249\t            0.82,\n250\t            0.017\n251\t          ],\n252\t          [\n253\t            \"Qwen3-0.6B-Base\",\n254\t            0.8,\n255\t            0.02\n256\t          ],\n257\t          [\n258\t            \"Pooled (all 5)\",\n259\t            0.83,\n260\t            0.012\n261\t          ]\n262\t        ]\n263\t      },\n264\t      {\n265\t        \"name\": \"Table 4b: Leave-One-Family-Out\",\n266\t        \"columns\": [\n267\t          \"Held-out Family\",\n268\t          \"Generators Held Out\",\n269\t          \"Macro-F1\"\n270\t        ],\n271\t        \"rows\": [\n272\t          [\n273\t            \"Pythia\",\n274\t            \"Pythia-2.8B + Pythia-1B\",\n275\t            0.71\n276\t          ],\n277\t          [\n278\t            \"Qwen\",\n279\t            \"Qwen3-1.7B + Qwen3-0.6B\",\n280\t            0.74\n281\t          ],\n282\t          [\n283\t            \"OLMo\",\n284\t            \"OLMo-2-0425-1B\",\n285\t            0.73\n286\t          ]\n287\t        ]\n288\t      }\n289\t    ],\n290\t    \"figures\": [],\n291\t    \"analysis\": \"LOGO F1=0.74 is the centerpiece of Claim 1. The ≥0.70 threshold is cleared with margin. Pythia-family slight advantage (0.76-0.77) is a limitation to discuss honestly: Pythia-410M reference model shares tokenizer/architecture with generators. Leave-one-family-out (0.71) still passes the 0.70 threshold, confirming depth signal is generation-process-intrinsic, not family-artifact. The gen-1 vs gen-2+ boundary remains the classification bottleneck across all generators.\\n\\n\\nLOGO mean 0.74 (within [0.70, 0.78] best range) reflects cross-generator generalization grounded in actual plan-001 performance (0.82 in-dist). The ~0.09 drop from in-distribution to LOGO is expected: each generator has its own distributional signature, and training without the held-out generator forces reliance on transfer of the depth-drift pattern. Pythia-family generators transfer best to each other (0.76-0.77) due to shared pretraining distribution (The Pile). Cross-family transfer (Pythia→Qwen, Pythia→OLMo) drops to 0.70-0.73 because the reference model (Pythia-410M) has different baseline expectations for text from non-Pythia generators, adding noise to features. Qwen3-0.6B-Base is hardest (0.70) because its small capacity produces lower-quality text that drifts faster at gen-1, making the depth ordering harder to predict from larger-model-trained classifiers. Leave-one-family-out for Pythia (train on OLMo+Qwen, test on Pythia-2.8B+1B) gives 0.71, confirming the signal is not Pythia-specific. Attrition slightly higher (4.8%) due to Qwen models' less stable generation at depth 4.\\n\"\n292\t  },\n293\t  {\n294\t    \"name\": \"cross_family_reference_model\",\n295\t    \"description\": \"Re-extract features using non-Pythia reference models (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B-Base) on plan_002 data. Key falsification test: does entropy_mean H2 drift persist with non-Pythia reference? If not, dual dynamics is Pythia-specific artifact.\\n\",\n296\t    \"results\": {\n297\t      \"pythia_410m_ref_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n298\t      \"olmo2_1b_ref_macro_f1\": 0.77,\n299\t      \"qwen3_06b_ref_macro_f1\": 0.74,\n300\t      \"entropy_mean_depth_rho_pythia\": -0.72,\n301\t      \"entropy_mean_depth_rho_olmo\": -0.65,\n302\t      \"entropy_mean_depth_rho_qwen\": -0.58,\n303\t      \"cross_ref_mean_rho\": -0.65,\n304\t      \"entropy_std_depth_rho_pythia\": -0.08,\n305\t      \"entropy_std_depth_rho_olmo\": -0.11,\n306\t      \"entropy_std_depth_rho_qwen\": -0.06,\n307\t      \"feature_correlation_pythia_olmo_entropy_mean\": 0.79,\n308\t      \"feature_correlation_pythia_qwen_entropy_mean\": 0.74,\n309\t      \"h2_drift_confirmed_all_refs\": true,\n310\t      \"h1_stationarity_confirmed_all_refs\": true,\n311\t      \"ensemble_3refs_macro_f1\": 0.85\n312\t    },\n313\t    \"tables\": [\n314\t      {\n315\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: Reference Model Comparison (pooled 5-generator data)\",\n316\t        \"columns\": [\n317\t          \"Reference Model\",\n318\t          \"Family\",\n319\t          \"Params\",\n320\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n321\t          \"gen-0 F1\",\n322\t          \"gen-1 F1\",\n323\t          \"gen-2+ F1\"\n324\t        ],\n325\t        \"rows\": [\n326\t          [\n327\t            \"Pythia-410M\",\n328\t            \"Pythia\",\n329\t            \"410M\",\n330\t            0.83,\n331\t            0.94,\n332\t            0.77,\n333\t            0.79\n334\t          ],\n335\t          [\n336\t            \"OLMo-2-1B\",\n337\t            \"OLMo\",\n338\t            \"1B\",\n339\t            0.77,\n340\t            0.89,\n341\t            0.7,\n342\t            0.72\n343\t          ],\n344\t          [\n345\t            \"Qwen3-0.6B-Base\",\n346\t            \"Qwen\",\n347\t            \"0.6B\",\n348\t            0.74,\n349\t            0.86,\n350\t            0.67,\n351\t            0.69\n352\t          ],\n353\t          [\n354\t            \"Ensemble (3 refs)\",\n355\t            \"Mixed\",\n356\t            \"—\",\n357\t            0.85,\n358\t            0.95,\n359\t            0.79,\n360\t            0.81\n361\t          ]\n362\t        ]\n363\t      },\n364\t      {\n365\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: Entropy Feature Depth Correlation (Spearman ρ) by Reference Model\",\n366\t        \"columns\": [\n367\t          \"Feature\",\n368\t          \"Pythia-410M\",\n369\t          \"OLMo-2-1B\",\n370\t          \"Qwen3-0.6B\",\n371\t          \"H1/H2 Classification\"\n372\t        ],\n373\t        \"rows\": [\n374\t          [\n375\t            \"entropy_mean\",\n376\t            -0.72,\n377\t            -0.65,\n378\t            -0.58,\n379\t            \"H2-drift\"\n380\t          ],\n381\t          [\n382\t            \"entropy_std\",\n383\t            -0.08,\n384\t            -0.11,\n385\t            -0.06,\n386\t            \"H1-stationary\"\n387\t          ],\n388\t          [\n389\t            \"entropy_max\",\n390\t            0.05,\n391\t            0.03,\n392\t            0.07,\n393\t            \"H1-stationary\"\n394\t          ],\n395\t          [\n396\t            \"entropy_min\",\n397\t            -0.1,\n398\t            -0.07,\n399\t            -0.09,\n400\t            \"H1-stationary\"\n401\t          ],\n402\t          [\n403\t            \"tail_decay\",\n404\t            0.04,\n405\t            0.06,\n406\t            0.03,\n407\t            \"H1-stationary\"\n408\t          ]\n409\t        ]\n410\t      },\n411\t      {\n412\t        \"name\": \"Table 6b: Cross-Reference Feature Correlation (Spearman ρ)\",\n413\t        \"columns\": [\n414\t          \"Feature\",\n415\t          \"Pythia↔OLMo\",\n416\t          \"Pythia↔Qwen\",\n417\t          \"OLMo↔Qwen\"\n418\t        ],\n419\t        \"rows\": [\n420\t          [\n421\t            \"entropy_mean\",\n422\t            0.79,\n423\t            0.74,\n424\t            0.77\n425\t          ],\n426\t          [\n427\t            \"entropy_std\",\n428\t            0.76,\n429\t            0.71,\n430\t            0.73\n431\t          ],\n432\t          [\n433\t            \"tail_decay\",\n434\t            0.68,\n435\t            0.63,\n436\t            0.66\n437\t          ]\n438\t        ]\n439\t      }\n440\t    ],\n441\t    \"figures\": [],\n442\t    \"analysis\": \"This experiment eliminates the most dangerous threat to the paper: that dual dynamics is a Pythia artifact. With all three reference models showing consistent H2 drift for entropy_mean (ρ range -0.58 to -0.72) and H1 stationarity for shape features (|ρ|<0.12), the level/shape framework is confirmed as generation-intrinsic. The 3-ref ensemble (0.85) exceeding any single ref confirms that diverse reference perspectives capture complementary drift information. For the paper, Table 6 is the most important evidence for the \\\"what it measures, not where it comes from\\\" argument.\\n\\n\\nentropy_mean ρ with depth ranges from -0.58 to -0.72 across reference models (mean -0.65, within [-0.75, -0.55] best range), confirming H2 drift is generation-intrinsic, not Pythia-specific. All three reference models detect monotonic entropy_mean decrease with depth because any well-trained LM assigns progressively higher probability (lower NLL) to deeper text — the text literally becomes more generic/predictable as generation recurses. Pythia-410M gives strongest signal (-0.72) because it was optimized for this pipeline and its 410M capacity is well-calibrated to detect drift (large enough to model human text, small enough to be \\\"surprised\\\" by generator deviations). OLMo (-0.65) and Qwen (-0.58) are noisier because their different pretraining distributions create different baselines. Crucially, entropy_std remains near-zero correlation with depth across all three models (|ρ|<0.12), confirming shape stationarity (H1) is also reference-model-agnostic. The dual dynamics is a property of the recursive generation process, not the measurement instrument. This is the key falsification test for Claim 2 — it passes.\\n\"\n443\t  },\n444\t  {\n445\t    \"name\": \"downstream_utility\",\n446\t    \"description\": \"Fine-tune OLMo-1B (avoiding Pythia-410M circularity) on depth-stratified subsets. ≥3 random seeds with bootstrap CIs. Compare depth-aware filtering vs FastDetectGPT and LD-Score binary baselines. Sample-count-matched comparison to control data size confound.\\n\",\n447\t    \"results\": {\n448\t      \"downstream_model\": \"OLMo-1B\",\n449\t      \"n_seeds\": 5,\n450\t      \"ppl_gen0_only_mean\": 31.2,\n451\t      \"ppl_gen0_only_ci\": [\n452\t        30.4,\n453\t        32.0\n454\t      ],\n455\t      \"ppl_gen1_only_mean\": 33.8,\n456\t      \"ppl_gen1_only_ci\": [\n457\t        32.9,\n458\t        34.7\n459\t      ],\n460\t      \"ppl_gen2_only_mean\": 38.5,\n461\t      \"ppl_gen2_only_ci\": [\n462\t        37.2,\n463\t        39.8\n464\t      ],\n465\t      \"ppl_gen3_only_mean\": 43.1,\n466\t      \"ppl_gen3_only_ci\": [\n467\t        41.5,\n468\t        44.7\n469\t      ],\n470\t      \"ppl_gen4_only_mean\": 47.6,\n471\t      \"ppl_gen4_only_ci\": [\n472\t        45.8,\n473\t        49.4\n474\t      ],\n475\t      \"depth_quality_spearman\": 0.74,\n476\t      \"ppl_depth_filtered_gen01_mean\": 32.1,\n477\t      \"ppl_depth_filtered_gen01_ci\": [\n478\t        31.3,\n479\t        32.9\n480\t      ],\n481\t      \"ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt_mean\": 36.8,\n482\t      \"ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt_ci\": [\n483\t        35.6,\n484\t        38.0\n485\t      ],\n486\t      \"ppl_binary_ldscore_mean\": 37.2,\n487\t      \"ppl_binary_ldscore_ci\": [\n488\t        35.9,\n489\t        38.5\n490\t      ],\n491\t      \"ppl_no_filter_mean\": 40.1,\n492\t      \"depth_filter_vs_binary_improvement_pct\": 13.2,\n493\t      \"data_retained_depth_filter_pct\": 44,\n494\t      \"data_retained_binary_pct\": 30,\n495\t      \"ppl_size_matched_depth_filter\": 33.0,\n496\t      \"ppl_size_matched_binary\": 37.0,\n497\t      \"size_matched_ci_non_overlapping\": true\n498\t    },\n499\t    \"tables\": [\n500\t      {\n501\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: Downstream Perplexity by Training Data Depth (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)\",\n502\t        \"columns\": [\n503\t          \"Training Data\",\n504\t          \"PPL Mean\",\n505\t          \"95% CI\",\n506\t          \"Samples\"\n507\t        ],\n508\t        \"rows\": [\n509\t          [\n510\t            \"gen-0 only\",\n511\t            31.2,\n512\t            \"[30.4, 32.0]\",\n513\t            \"~4800\"\n514\t          ],\n515\t          [\n516\t            \"gen-1 only\",\n517\t            33.8,\n518\t            \"[32.9, 34.7]\",\n519\t            \"~4700\"\n520\t          ],\n521\t          [\n522\t            \"gen-2 only\",\n523\t            38.5,\n524\t            \"[37.2, 39.8]\",\n525\t            \"~4400\"\n526\t          ],\n527\t          [\n528\t            \"gen-3 only\",\n529\t            43.1,\n530\t            \"[41.5, 44.7]\",\n531\t            \"~4100\"\n532\t          ],\n533\t          [\n534\t            \"gen-4 only\",\n535\t            47.6,\n536\t            \"[45.8, 49.4]\",\n537\t            \"~3800\"\n538\t          ]\n539\t        ]\n540\t      },\n541\t      {\n542\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: Depth-Aware vs Binary Filtering (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)\",\n543\t        \"columns\": [\n544\t          \"Method\",\n545\t          \"PPL Mean\",\n546\t          \"95% CI\",\n547\t          \"Data Retained\",\n548\t          \"Δ vs No-Filter\"\n549\t        ],\n550\t        \"rows\": [\n551\t          [\n552\t            \"Depth-filtered (gen-0+1)\",\n553\t            32.1,\n554\t            \"[31.3, 32.9]\",\n555\t            \"44%\",\n556\t            \"-20.0%\"\n557\t          ],\n558\t          [\n559\t            \"FastDetectGPT binary\",\n560\t            36.8,\n561\t            \"[35.6, 38.0]\",\n562\t            \"30%\",\n563\t            \"-8.2%\"\n564\t          ],\n565\t          [\n566\t            \"LD-Score binary\",\n567\t            37.2,\n568\t            \"[35.9, 38.5]\",\n569\t            \"28%\",\n570\t            \"-7.2%\"\n571\t          ],\n572\t          [\n573\t            \"No filtering\",\n574\t            40.1,\n575\t            \"[38.8, 41.4]\",\n576\t            \"100%\",\n577\t            \"—\"\n578\t          ]\n579\t        ]\n580\t      },\n581\t      {\n582\t        \"name\": \"Table 8b: Size-Matched Comparison (subsample to equal data count)\",\n583\t        \"columns\": [\n584\t          \"Method\",\n585\t          \"Matched Count\",\n586\t          \"PPL Mean\",\n587\t          \"95% CI\"\n588\t        ],\n589\t        \"rows\": [\n590\t          [\n591\t            \"Depth-filtered (subsampled)\",\n592\t            \"~5500\",\n593\t            33.0,\n594\t            \"[32.1, 33.9]\"\n595\t          ],\n596\t          [\n597\t            \"Binary-filtered\",\n598\t            \"~5500\",\n599\t            37.0,\n600\t            \"[35.8, 38.2]\"\n601\t          ],\n602\t          [\n603\t            \"CIs non-overlapping\",\n604\t            \"—\",\n605\t            \"YES\",\n606\t            \"—\"\n607\t          ]\n608\t        ]\n609\t      }\n610\t    ],\n611\t    \"figures\": [],\n612\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the practical punchline. The 13.2% improvement with CIs non-overlapping under size-matching establishes that depth-aware filtering provides genuine data quality advantage, not just a data quantity effect. gen-1 text is \\\"good enough\\\" for training (PPL 33.8 vs gen-0's 31.2 — 8% gap), but gen-2+ is harmful (PPL 38.5+, >23% worse than gen-0). Binary detectors force an all-or-nothing choice and discard useful gen-1 content. This directly validates Claim 3 under the strengthened protocol (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds, CIs, size-matched).\\n\\n\\nOLMo-1B perplexity increases monotonically with training depth (31.2→47.6) because deeper text has compounded distributional drift from human text — fine-tuning on it shifts OLMo-1B's distribution away from the human test set. The absolute PPL values are higher than the previous dream (which used Pythia-410M) because OLMo-1B is a stronger model with higher baseline expectations. Spearman ρ=0.74 (within [0.65, 0.85]) reflects individual sample variance within depth classes. Depth-aware filtering (keep gen-0+gen-1, PPL=32.1) outperforms binary baselines (~36.8-37.2) by 13.2% (within [8, 20] range) because binary detectors discard all synthetic text — including useful gen-1 content that is distributionally near-human. Binary filters retain only ~30% of data (mostly gen-0) while depth filtering retains 44% (gen-0 + gen-1), providing more and better training data. The size-matched comparison is critical: even when subsampling depth-filtered data to match binary filter count, depth filtering still wins (33.0 vs 37.0, CIs non-overlapping) — confirming the advantage is data quality, not quantity. Using OLMo-1B eliminates the Pythia reference model circularity concern. 5-seed bootstrap CIs are tight enough to establish significance.\\n\"\n613\t  },\n614\t  {\n615\t    \"name\": \"prompt_type_comparison\",\n616\t    \"description\": \"Compare continuation vs elaboration vs inspired_rewriting chains. 1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 5 depths × 3 prompt types.\\n\",\n617\t    \"results\": {\n618\t      \"continuation_macro_f1\": 0.82,\n619\t      \"elaboration_macro_f1\": 0.77,\n620\t      \"inspired_rewriting_macro_f1\": 0.69,\n621\t      \"continuation_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.17,\n622\t      \"elaboration_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.13,\n623\t      \"inspired_rewriting_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.08,\n624\t      \"cross_prompt_type_macro_f1\": 0.71,\n625\t      \"prompt_drift_rate_f1_spearman\": 0.93\n626\t    },\n627\t    \"tables\": [\n628\t      {\n629\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: Depth Classification by Prompt Type (Pythia-2.8B)\",\n630\t        \"columns\": [\n631\t          \"Prompt Type\",\n632\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n633\t          \"gen-0 F1\",\n634\t          \"gen-1 F1\",\n635\t          \"gen-2+ F1\",\n636\t          \"Drift Rate (nats/depth)\"\n637\t        ],\n638\t        \"rows\": [\n639\t          [\n640\t            \"Continuation\",\n641\t            0.82,\n642\t            0.94,\n643\t            0.76,\n644\t            0.78,\n645\t            0.17\n646\t          ],\n647\t          [\n648\t            \"Elaboration\",\n649\t            0.77,\n650\t            0.9,\n651\t            0.7,\n652\t            0.72,\n653\t            0.13\n654\t          ],\n655\t          [\n656\t            \"Inspired rewriting\",\n657\t            0.69,\n658\t            0.84,\n659\t            0.61,\n660\t            0.63,\n661\t            0.08\n662\t          ],\n663\t          [\n664\t            \"Cross-prompt LOGO\",\n665\t            0.71,\n666\t            0.85,\n667\t            0.63,\n668\t            0.65,\n669\t            \"—\"\n670\t          ]\n671\t        ]\n672\t      }\n673\t    ],\n674\t    \"figures\": [],\n675\t    \"analysis\": \"The ordering (continuation > elaboration > inspired rewriting) aligns with theory: semantic coupling determines drift accumulation rate. This is a supporting result — it enriches the paper but isn't strictly necessary for the core claims. Best used to show that depth signal is graded and mechanism-consistent.\\n\\n\\nContinuation preserves strongest depth signal (0.82) because each step directly extends previous output, maximizing cumulative drift. Elaboration (0.77) injects new semantic direction via prompt instruction, partially resetting drift per step. Inspired rewriting (0.69) provides most semantic freedom — the model can re-anchor to its pretraining distribution at each step, yielding weakest depth accumulation. The strong correlation between per-depth entropy drift rate and F1 (ρ=0.93) confirms the theoretical prediction: more constrained generation → faster drift accumulation → stronger depth signal. Cross-prompt transfer (0.71) is moderate because drift signatures are partially shared but rate differences create distribution shift. This result enriches the theoretical story without changing the core claims.\\n\"\n676\t  },\n677\t  {\n678\t    \"name\": \"paraphrastic_comparison\",\n679\t    \"description\": \"Parallel paraphrasing chains (same 1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 5 depths) vs continuation chains. Direct test of attractor convergence (H1) vs monotonic divergence (H2).\\n\",\n680\t    \"results\": {\n681\t      \"paraphrase_macro_f1_3class\": 0.55,\n682\t      \"paraphrase_gen0_f1\": 0.79,\n683\t      \"paraphrase_gen1_f1\": 0.43,\n684\t      \"paraphrase_gen2plus_f1\": 0.44,\n685\t      \"continuation_macro_f1_3class\": 0.82,\n686\t      \"paraphrase_entropy_drift_depth3\": 0.05,\n687\t      \"continuation_entropy_drift_depth3\": 0.48,\n688\t      \"paraphrase_cycle_detection_pct\": 64,\n689\t      \"ks_statistic_para_vs_cont_depth3\": 0.39,\n690\t      \"ks_p_value\": 1e-05\n691\t    },\n692\t    \"tables\": [\n693\t      {\n694\t        \"name\": \"Table 10: Paraphrastic vs Continuation Chain Dynamics\",\n695\t        \"columns\": [\n696\t          \"Chain Type\",\n697\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n698\t          \"Entropy Drift @depth3\",\n699\t          \"Cycle Detection %\",\n700\t          \"KS D\"\n701\t        ],\n702\t        \"rows\": [\n703\t          [\n704\t            \"Continuation\",\n705\t            0.82,\n706\t            \"0.48 nats\",\n707\t            \"<5%\",\n708\t            \"—\"\n709\t          ],\n710\t          [\n711\t            \"Paraphrasing\",\n712\t            0.55,\n713\t            \"0.05 nats\",\n714\t            \"64%\",\n715\t            \"—\"\n716\t          ],\n717\t          [\n718\t            \"KS test (depth-3)\",\n719\t            \"—\",\n720\t            \"—\",\n721\t            \"—\",\n722\t            \"0.39 (p<0.001)\"\n723\t          ]\n724\t        ]\n725\t      }\n726\t    ],\n727\t    \"figures\": [],\n728\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the conceptual anchor connecting our empirical finding to the attractor cycle literature. The clean separation validates why we focus on non-paraphrastic chains and why our approach succeeds where paraphrasing-based analysis would fail at depth estimation beyond ~2 classes.\\n\\n\\nParaphrastic chains show weak depth signal (F1=0.55) because the semantic-preservation constraint creates attractor dynamics — distributional features converge after ~2 steps, consistent with ACL 2025 attractor cycle theory. By depth 3, paraphrastic entropy drift has nearly plateaued (0.05 nats) while continuation drift continues (0.48 nats). The 64% cycle detection rate in paraphrastic chains (depth d closer to d-2 than d-1 in feature space) directly corroborates 2-period attractor cycle predictions. The KS test (D=0.39, p<0.001) provides formal evidence that paraphrastic and continuation depth-3 distributions are distinct. This elevates the paper from \\\"a depth classifier\\\" to \\\"an empirical test of generation dynamics\\\" by connecting to established theoretical literature. Our H2 divergence for non-paraphrastic chains and H1 convergence for paraphrastic chains are two sides of the same coin — the presence or absence of semantic constraint determines whether drift accumulates or converges.\\n\"\n729\t  },\n730\t  {\n731\t    \"name\": \"cross_model_chains\",\n732\t    \"description\": \"Cross-model chains: different generator per depth step (randomly sampled from 5 generators). 1000 seeds × 5 depths. Tests ecological validity — real contamination uses diverse generators.\\n\",\n733\t    \"results\": {\n734\t      \"cross_model_macro_f1\": 0.71,\n735\t      \"single_model_macro_f1_mean\": 0.82,\n736\t      \"cross_model_gen0_f1\": 0.88,\n737\t      \"cross_model_gen1_f1\": 0.64,\n738\t      \"cross_model_gen2plus_f1\": 0.63,\n739\t      \"cross_vs_single_delta\": -0.11,\n740\t      \"cross_model_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.12\n741\t    },\n742\t    \"tables\": [\n743\t      {\n744\t        \"name\": \"Table 11: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chain Comparison\",\n745\t        \"columns\": [\n746\t          \"Chain Type\",\n747\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n748\t          \"gen-0 F1\",\n749\t          \"gen-1 F1\",\n750\t          \"gen-2+ F1\"\n751\t        ],\n752\t        \"rows\": [\n753\t          [\n754\t            \"Single-model (mean of 5)\",\n755\t            0.82,\n756\t            0.94,\n757\t            0.76,\n758\t            0.78\n759\t          ],\n760\t          [\n761\t            \"Cross-model (random switch)\",\n762\t            0.71,\n763\t            0.88,\n764\t            0.64,\n765\t            0.63\n766\t          ],\n767\t          [\n768\t            \"Δ\",\n769\t            -0.11,\n770\t            -0.06,\n771\t            -0.12,\n772\t            -0.15\n773\t          ]\n774\t        ]\n775\t      }\n776\t    ],\n777\t    \"figures\": [],\n778\t    \"analysis\": \"The 11-point drop is expected and honestly bounded. For data curation, the key utility is still maintained: the method separates human from synthetic (gen-0 F1=0.88) even under realistic multi-generator contamination. Finer depth estimation within synthetic text is where accuracy degrades — an honest limitation for the Discussion section.\\n\\n\\nCross-model chains lose ~11 points from single-model (0.71 vs 0.82) because generator switching introduces distributional discontinuities at each step — the new generator's idiosyncratic signature partially masks the accumulated depth drift. However, F1=0.71 still far exceeds chance (0.33) because the underlying drift phenomenon persists: regardless of which generator produces each step, cumulative deviation from human text distribution still increases with depth. gen-0 remains well-separated (0.88) — human text is fundamentally distinct from any generator. The gen-1/gen-2+ boundary degrades most (0.64/0.63) because generator-switching noise creates feature-space overlap between these classes. Entropy drift rate (0.12 nats/depth) is lower than single-model (0.17) because each generator transition partially resets the drift trajectory. This is the honest cost of ecological validity — reported transparently, it strengthens the paper.\\n\"\n779\t  },\n780\t  {\n781\t    \"name\": \"multi_domain_seeds\",\n782\t    \"description\": \"4 domains: Wikipedia, news (CNN/DailyMail), scientific (S2ORC), creative (WritingPrompts). 500 seeds per domain × 5 generators × 5 depths.\\n\",\n783\t    \"results\": {\n784\t      \"wikipedia_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n785\t      \"news_macro_f1\": 0.8,\n786\t      \"scientific_macro_f1\": 0.78,\n787\t      \"creative_macro_f1\": 0.74,\n788\t      \"cross_domain_logo_f1\": 0.74,\n789\t      \"pooled_all_domains_f1\": 0.81,\n790\t      \"domain_f1_variance\": 0.0012\n791\t    },\n792\t    \"tables\": [\n793\t      {\n794\t        \"name\": \"Table 12: Multi-Domain Depth Classification\",\n795\t        \"columns\": [\n796\t          \"Domain\",\n797\t          \"Seeds\",\n798\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n799\t          \"gen-0 F1\",\n800\t          \"gen-1 F1\",\n801\t          \"gen-2+ F1\"\n802\t        ],\n803\t        \"rows\": [\n804\t          [\n805\t            \"Wikipedia\",\n806\t            500,\n807\t            0.83,\n808\t            0.94,\n809\t            0.77,\n810\t            0.79\n811\t          ],\n812\t          [\n813\t            \"News\",\n814\t            500,\n815\t            0.8,\n816\t            0.92,\n817\t            0.74,\n818\t            0.76\n819\t          ],\n820\t          [\n821\t            \"Scientific\",\n822\t            500,\n823\t            0.78,\n824\t            0.9,\n825\t            0.71,\n826\t            0.73\n827\t          ],\n828\t          [\n829\t            \"Creative\",\n830\t            500,\n831\t            0.74,\n832\t            0.86,\n833\t            0.67,\n834\t            0.69\n835\t          ],\n836\t          [\n837\t            \"Pooled (all)\",\n838\t            2000,\n839\t            0.81,\n840\t            0.92,\n841\t            0.74,\n842\t            0.76\n843\t          ],\n844\t          [\n845\t            \"Leave-one-domain-out\",\n846\t            \"—\",\n847\t            0.74,\n848\t            0.87,\n849\t            0.67,\n850\t            0.69\n851\t          ]\n852\t        ]\n853\t      }\n854\t    ],\n855\t    \"figures\": [],\n856\t    \"analysis\": \"Domain generalization confirms distributional drift is not a Wikipedia artifact. The factual-to- creative ordering is interpretable and provides practitioner guidance: depth estimation is most reliable for factual domains. Four domains with LOGO evaluation directly addresses RC6.\\n\\n\\nWikipedia performs best (0.83) as it matches the MVP development setting with structured, information-dense text that generators struggle to faithfully continue. News (0.80) has similar factual density. Scientific text (0.78) has specialized vocabulary that partially masks depth drift in surface features, though reference features still detect it. Creative writing is hardest (0.74) because generators produce more fluent, human-like creative text — the distributional gap between human and generated creative text is smaller, reducing overall drift magnitude. The low F1 variance across domains (0.0012) indicates depth signal is a robust, domain-general phenomenon. Cross-domain LOGO (0.74) confirms generalization without domain-specific calibration. This addresses RC6 (ecological validity) by showing the approach works across diverse text types.\\n\"\n857\t  },\n858\t  {\n859\t    \"name\": \"finer_grained_ordinal\",\n860\t    \"description\": \"5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on plan_002 data. Tests depth resolution ceiling for non-paraphrastic chains.\\n\",\n861\t    \"results\": {\n862\t      \"ordinal_5class_macro_f1\": 0.58,\n863\t      \"ordinal_gen0_f1\": 0.91,\n864\t      \"ordinal_gen1_f1\": 0.65,\n865\t      \"ordinal_gen2_f1\": 0.48,\n866\t      \"ordinal_gen3_f1\": 0.44,\n867\t      \"ordinal_gen4_f1\": 0.46,\n868\t      \"ordinal_mae\": 0.62,\n869\t      \"ordinal_spearman_rho\": 0.81,\n870\t      \"adjacent_class_confusion_pct\": 74\n871\t    },\n872\t    \"tables\": [\n873\t      {\n874\t        \"name\": \"Table 13: 5-Class Ordinal Depth Classification\",\n875\t        \"columns\": [\n876\t          \"Class\",\n877\t          \"Precision\",\n878\t          \"Recall\",\n879\t          \"F1\"\n880\t        ],\n881\t        \"rows\": [\n882\t          [\n883\t            \"gen-0\",\n884\t            0.92,\n885\t            0.9,\n886\t            0.91\n887\t          ],\n888\t          [\n889\t            \"gen-1\",\n890\t            0.63,\n891\t            0.67,\n892\t            0.65\n893\t          ],\n894\t          [\n895\t            \"gen-2\",\n896\t            0.46,\n897\t            0.5,\n898\t            0.48\n899\t          ],\n900\t          [\n901\t            \"gen-3\",\n902\t            0.43,\n903\t            0.45,\n904\t            0.44\n905\t          ],\n906\t          [\n907\t            \"gen-4\",\n908\t            0.48,\n909\t            0.44,\n910\t            0.46\n911\t          ],\n912\t          [\n913\t            \"Macro\",\n914\t            \"—\",\n915\t            \"—\",\n916\t            0.58\n917\t          ]\n918\t        ]\n919\t      },\n920\t      {\n921\t        \"name\": \"Table 13b: Ordinal Error Statistics\",\n922\t        \"columns\": [\n923\t          \"Metric\",\n924\t          \"Value\"\n925\t        ],\n926\t        \"rows\": [\n927\t          [\n928\t            \"MAE\",\n929\t            0.62\n930\t          ],\n931\t          [\n932\t            \"Spearman ρ (pred vs true)\",\n933\t            0.81\n934\t          ],\n935\t          [\n936\t            \"Adjacent-class confusion\",\n937\t            \"74%\"\n938\t          ]\n939\t        ]\n940\t      }\n941\t    ],\n942\t    \"figures\": [],\n943\t    \"analysis\": \"5-class F1=0.58 characterizes the practical resolution ceiling. The ordinal structure (ρ=0.81) is the more important metric than exact accuracy — for curation, knowing approximate depth is more useful than exact classification. This result differentiates from attractor theory's ~2-class ceiling for paraphrasing.\\n\\n\\n5-class F1=0.58 (within [0.50, 0.65] best range) reflects the resolution ceiling of 7 distributional features. gen-0 remains easy (0.91), gen-1 moderate (0.65), but gen-2/3/4 have heavily overlapping feature distributions (F1=0.44-0.48). Drift accumulates monotonically but with diminishing marginal signal per step — the distributional distance between gen-3 and gen-4 is smaller than between gen-0 and gen-1 because the generation process approaches a distributional attractor. However, the high Spearman ρ (0.81) and the 74% adjacent-class error pattern confirm that the classifier preserves ordinal structure: mistakes are off-by-one, not off-by-three. This supports ordinal regression and directly addresses RC3: non-paraphrastic chains support at least 3 clean classes (vs ~2 for paraphrasing), and the ordinal signal extends to 5 classes even if exact classification weakens. For data curation, coarse buckets (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) are more useful anyway.\\n\"\n944\t  },\n945\t  {\n946\t    \"name\": \"human_edit_robustness\",\n947\t    \"description\": \"Robustness to simulated edits: random word replacement (5/10/20%) and LLM-simulated light editing.\\n\",\n948\t    \"results\": {\n949\t      \"no_edit_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n950\t      \"random_5pct_f1\": 0.8,\n951\t      \"random_10pct_f1\": 0.75,\n952\t      \"random_20pct_f1\": 0.63,\n953\t      \"llm_light_edit_f1\": 0.73,\n954\t      \"degradation_slope_per_10pct\": -0.1\n955\t    },\n956\t    \"tables\": [\n957\t      {\n958\t        \"name\": \"Table 14: Robustness to Simulated Edits\",\n959\t        \"columns\": [\n960\t          \"Edit Type\",\n961\t          \"Edit Rate\",\n962\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n963\t          \"Δ from Baseline\"\n964\t        ],\n965\t        \"rows\": [\n966\t          [\n967\t            \"None\",\n968\t            \"0%\",\n969\t            0.83,\n970\t            \"—\"\n971\t          ],\n972\t          [\n973\t            \"Random word replacement\",\n974\t            \"5%\",\n975\t            0.8,\n976\t            \"-0.03\"\n977\t          ],\n978\t          [\n979\t            \"Random word replacement\",\n980\t            \"10%\",\n981\t            0.75,\n982\t            \"-0.08\"\n983\t          ],\n984\t          [\n985\t            \"Random word replacement\",\n986\t            \"20%\",\n987\t            0.63,\n988\t            \"-0.20\"\n989\t          ],\n990\t          [\n991\t            \"LLM light editing\",\n992\t            \"~8-12%\",\n993\t            0.73,\n994\t            \"-0.10\"\n995\t          ]\n996\t        ]\n997\t      }\n998\t    ],\n999\t    \"figures\": [],\n1000\t    \"analysis\": \"Practical takeaway: depth estimation remains usable (F1>0.70) at realistic editing levels (≤10%). Heavy editing (20%+) significantly degrades performance — an honest limitation. The method targets unedited or lightly post-processed synthetic text, which is the majority of web-scraped LLM output.\\n\\n\\n5% random replacement has minimal impact (Δ=-0.03) because uniform token noise preserves relative distributional differences between depth classes. At 10% (0.75, Δ=-0.08), noise begins masking depth-specific features as random words inject unpredictable NLL values. At 20% (0.63, Δ=-0.20), substantial corruption disrupts the fingerprint significantly but stays above chance. LLM light editing (0.73, comparable to 10% random) changes surface statistics but largely preserves distributional structure — edited text occupies similar reference-model feature space because rephrasing doesn't fundamentally change what the text is about. The ~linear degradation (-0.10 F1 per 10% edit rate) provides a practitioner rule-of-thumb for estimating signal reliability at different editing intensities. These results address RC5: the method is viable for lightly edited synthetic text (the common case in web data).\\n\"\n1001\t  },\n1002\t  {\n1003\t    \"name\": \"ld_score_feature\",\n1004\t    \"description\": \"Add LD-Score as feature. Pre-registered prediction (RC7): LD-Score is a level feature exhibiting H2 drift.\\n\",\n1005\t    \"results\": {\n1006\t      \"full_plus_ld_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n1007\t      \"ld_score_only_f1\": 0.55,\n1008\t      \"ld_score_permutation_importance\": 0.05,\n1009\t      \"ld_score_depth_rho\": -0.52,\n1010\t      \"ld_score_h2_confirmed\": true,\n1011\t      \"full_plus_ld_vs_full_delta\": 0.01\n1012\t    },\n1013\t    \"tables\": [\n1014\t      {\n1015\t        \"name\": \"Table S1: LD-Score Feature Integration\",\n1016\t        \"columns\": [\n1017\t          \"Feature Set\",\n1018\t          \"Macro-F1\",\n1019\t          \"Δ from Full\",\n1020\t          \"LD-Score ρ with Depth\"\n1021\t        ],\n1022\t        \"rows\": [\n1023\t          [\n1024\t            \"Full (7 features)\",\n1025\t            0.82,\n1026\t            \"—\",\n1027\t            \"—\"\n1028\t          ],\n1029\t          [\n1030\t            \"Full + LD-Score\",\n1031\t            0.83,\n1032\t            \"+0.01\",\n1033\t            \"—\"\n1034\t          ],\n1035\t          [\n1036\t            \"LD-Score only\",\n1037\t            0.55,\n1038\t            \"-0.27\",\n1039\t            \"-0.52 (H2 confirmed)\"\n1040\t          ]\n1041\t        ]\n1042\t      }\n1043\t    ],\n1044\t    \"figures\": [],\n1045\t    \"analysis\": \"The pre-registered prediction strengthens the paper's credibility. LD-Score drifts as a level feature, exactly as the framework predicts. But its practical value is minimal — the existing 7 features capture the same signal more efficiently. This is a useful negative-ish result that reinforces the sufficiency of the current feature set.\\n\\n\\nLD-Score (character-level distributional signature) exhibits H2 drift (ρ=-0.52) confirming the pre-registered prediction: it is a level feature sensitive to vocabulary/character frequency shifts that accumulate with depth. However, adding it to the full feature set gives only marginal improvement (Δ=+0.01) because its signal is largely redundant with surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) — both measure vocabulary impoverishment at different granularities. LD-Score alone (F1=0.55) is weaker than surface features alone (0.76) because character-level statistics are noisier proxies for depth than word-level statistics. The pre-registered prediction being confirmed is a small but clean validation of the level/shape framework: we predicted LD-Score's behavior class before observing it, and the prediction held. This belongs in supplementary material.\\n\"\n1046\t  },\n1047\t  {\n1048\t    \"name\": \"end_to_end_classifier\",\n1049\t    \"description\": \"QLoRA fine-tuned Qwen3-0.6B-Base for end-to-end depth classification. Compares learned representations vs handcrafted distributional features.\\n\",\n1050\t    \"results\": {\n1051\t      \"qlora_in_dist_macro_f1\": 0.8,\n1052\t      \"qlora_gen0_f1\": 0.93,\n1053\t      \"qlora_gen1_f1\": 0.74,\n1054\t      \"qlora_gen2plus_f1\": 0.75,\n1055\t      \"handcrafted_in_dist_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n1056\t      \"qlora_cross_gen_logo_f1\": 0.66,\n1057\t      \"handcrafted_cross_gen_logo_f1\": 0.74,\n1058\t      \"qlora_training_gpu_hours\": 14,\n1059\t      \"handcrafted_training_minutes\": 3\n1060\t    },\n1061\t    \"tables\": [\n1062\t      {\n1063\t        \"name\": \"Table S2: Feature-Based vs End-to-End Comparison\",\n1064\t        \"columns\": [\n1065\t          \"Method\",\n1066\t          \"In-Dist F1\",\n1067\t          \"Cross-Gen LOGO F1\",\n1068\t          \"Training Cost\"\n1069\t        ],\n1070\t        \"rows\": [\n1071\t          [\n1072\t            \"XGBoost + handcrafted\",\n1073\t            0.83,\n1074\t            0.74,\n1075\t            \"3 min\"\n1076\t          ],\n1077\t          [\n1078\t            \"QLoRA Qwen3-0.6B\",\n1079\t            0.8,\n1080\t            0.66,\n1081\t            \"14 GPU·h\"\n1082\t          ],\n1083\t          [\n1084\t            \"Δ (handcrafted − QLoRA)\",\n1085\t            \"+0.03\",\n1086\t            \"+0.08\",\n1087\t            \"—\"\n1088\t          ]\n1089\t        ]\n1090\t      }\n1091\t    ],\n1092\t    \"figures\": [],\n1093\t    \"analysis\": \"Handcrafted features outperform, generalize better, and are 4000× cheaper. The cross-gen gap (0.08 vs 0.03 in-dist) is the key diagnostic: QLoRA takes generator-specific shortcuts. This reinforces the contribution positioning — the novelty is the problem formulation and empirical finding, not the classifier architecture.\\n\\n\\nThe QLoRA model achieves competitive in-distribution F1 (0.80) because transformers can implicitly learn distributional features from raw text. However, it underperforms handcrafted features (0.83) for two reasons: (1) handcrafted features distill the reference model's perspective into compact statistics that directly measure drift, while QLoRA must learn this implicitly; (2) XGBoost on 7 features is harder to overfit than a 0.6B model on ~24K examples. The larger cross-gen gap (0.66 vs 0.74) reveals that QLoRA memorizes generator-specific text patterns more than the handcrafted features, which are designed to measure generator-agnostic distributional drift. The 4000× training cost difference (14 GPU-hours vs 3 minutes) makes the feature-based approach clearly preferable. This validates the paper's focus on interpretable features over end-to-end learning and directly answers \\\"why not just fine-tune a model?\\\"\\n\"\n1094\t  },\n1095\t  {\n1096\t    \"name\": \"text_length_control\",\n1097\t    \"description\": \"Address length confound (RC10): (A) Length distribution analysis by depth. (B) Length-normalized surface features (TTR, hapax on fixed 200-token windows). (C) Length-matched subsample comparison.\\n\",\n1098\t    \"results\": {\n1099\t      \"mean_length_gen0_tokens\": 285,\n1100\t      \"mean_length_gen1_tokens\": 268,\n1101\t      \"mean_length_gen2_tokens\": 253,\n1102\t      \"mean_length_gen3_tokens\": 244,\n1103\t      \"mean_length_gen4_tokens\": 238,\n1104\t      \"length_depth_spearman\": -0.35,\n1105\t      \"length_controlled_full_f1\": 0.8,\n1106\t      \"length_controlled_surface_only_f1\": 0.74,\n1107\t      \"original_full_f1\": 0.82,\n1108\t      \"original_surface_only_f1\": 0.76,\n1109\t      \"length_controlled_f1_delta_full\": -0.02,\n1110\t      \"length_controlled_f1_delta_surface\": -0.02,\n1111\t      \"length_normalized_ttr_depth_rho\": -0.41,\n1112\t      \"original_ttr_depth_rho\": -0.48,\n1113\t      \"length_matched_full_f1\": 0.81\n1114\t    },\n1115\t    \"tables\": [\n1116\t      {\n1117\t        \"name\": \"Table S3: Text Length Distribution by Depth\",\n1118\t        \"columns\": [\n1119\t          \"Depth\",\n1120\t          \"Mean Length (tokens)\",\n1121\t          \"Std\",\n1122\t          \"Median\"\n1123\t        ],\n1124\t        \"rows\": [\n1125\t          [\n1126\t            \"gen-0\",\n1127\t            285,\n1128\t            82,\n1129\t            271\n1130\t          ],\n1131\t          [\n1132\t            \"gen-1\",\n1133\t            268,\n1134\t            75,\n1135\t            254\n1136\t          ],\n1137\t          [\n1138\t            \"gen-2\",\n1139\t            253,\n1140\t            71,\n1141\t            240\n1142\t          ],\n1143\t          [\n1144\t            \"gen-3\",\n1145\t            244,\n1146\t            68,\n1147\t            232\n1148\t          ],\n1149\t          [\n1150\t            \"gen-4\",\n1151\t            238,\n1152\t            66,\n1153\t            226\n1154\t          ]\n1155\t        ]\n1156\t      },\n1157\t      {\n1158\t        \"name\": \"Table S4: Length-Controlled Feature Evaluation\",\n1159\t        \"columns\": [\n1160\t          \"Condition\",\n1161\t          \"Full F1\",\n1162\t          \"Surface F1\",\n1163\t          \"Δ Full\",\n1164\t          \"Δ Surface\"\n1165\t        ],\n1166\t        \"rows\": [\n1167\t          [\n1168\t            \"Original\",\n1169\t            0.82,\n1170\t            0.76,\n1171\t            \"—\",\n1172\t            \"—\"\n1173\t          ],\n1174\t          [\n1175\t            \"Length-normalized (200-token window)\",\n1176\t            0.8,\n1177\t            0.74,\n1178\t            \"-0.02\",\n1179\t            \"-0.02\"\n1180\t          ],\n1181\t          [\n1182\t            \"Length-matched subsample\",\n1183\t            0.81,\n1184\t            0.75,\n1185\t            \"-0.01\",\n1186\t            \"-0.01\"\n1187\t          ]\n1188\t        ]\n1189\t      },\n1190\t      {\n1191\t        \"name\": \"Table S4b: Length-Controlled Feature Depth Correlation\",\n1192\t        \"columns\": [\n1193\t          \"Feature\",\n1194\t          \"Original ρ\",\n1195\t          \"Length-Normalized ρ\",\n1196\t          \"Signal Retained\"\n1197\t        ],\n1198\t        \"rows\": [\n1199\t          [\n1200\t            \"ttr\",\n1201\t            -0.48,\n1202\t            -0.41,\n1203\t            \"85%\"\n1204\t          ],\n1205\t          [\n1206\t            \"hapax_ratio\",\n1207\t            -0.44,\n1208\t            -0.38,\n1209\t            \"86%\"\n1210\t          ],\n1211\t          [\n1212\t            \"entropy_mean\",\n1213\t            -0.72,\n1214\t            -0.71,\n1215\t            \"99%\"\n1216\t          ]\n1217\t        ]\n1218\t      }\n1219\t    ],\n1220\t    \"figures\": [],\n1221\t    \"analysis\": \"Length confound is real but small: ~15% of surface feature signal is length-driven, ~85% is genuine depth signal. Reference features (entropy_mean) are essentially unaffected (99% signal retained) because NLL statistics are length-invariant. The -0.02 F1 delta for both full and surface features confirms the confound doesn't drive the main results. This must be in the paper to preempt RC10 and maintain credibility.\\n\\n\\nText length decreases modestly with depth (285→238 tokens, ρ=-0.35) because generators tend to produce slightly shorter continuations at deeper steps due to vocabulary impoverishment and repetition. TTR and hapax_ratio are mechanically length-dependent (shorter texts have higher TTR), which could inflate surface feature depth signal. However, length-controlled analysis shows minimal F1 drop: full features decline only 0.02 (0.82→0.80, within [-0.03, 0.0] best range) and surface features also drop only 0.02 (0.76→0.74). This confirms that surface features are tracking genuine vocabulary impoverishment, not length artifacts. Length-normalized TTR still shows significant depth correlation (ρ=-0.41 vs original -0.48), meaning ~85% of the signal survives length control. Length-matched subsampling (equalizing length distributions across depths) yields F1=0.81, confirming the classifier is not exploiting length as a shortcut. This is a critical confound control that must appear in the main paper — reviewers will look for it.\\n\"\n1222\t  },\n1223\t  {\n1224\t    \"name\": \"large_scale_generator\",\n1225\t    \"description\": \"Add Qwen3-8B-Base (or similar 7B+ model) to test scale generalization. 1000 seeds × 5 depths on RTX 5090 (32GB). Addresses RC9.\\n\",\n1226\t    \"results\": {\n1227\t      \"qwen3_8b_in_dist_macro_f1\": 0.78,\n1228\t      \"qwen3_8b_gen0_f1\": 0.91,\n1229\t      \"qwen3_8b_gen1_f1\": 0.71,\n1230\t      \"qwen3_8b_gen2plus_f1\": 0.73,\n1231\t      \"qwen3_8b_logo_f1\": 0.71,\n1232\t      \"qwen3_8b_entropy_mean_depth_rho\": -0.58,\n1233\t      \"qwen3_8b_ttr_depth_rho\": -0.39,\n1234\t      \"qwen3_8b_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.12,\n1235\t      \"small_generators_mean_drift_per_depth\": 0.17,\n1236\t      \"drift_ratio_7b_vs_small\": 0.71,\n1237\t      \"h2_confirmed_7b\": true,\n1238\t      \"h1_confirmed_7b\": true\n1239\t    },\n1240\t    \"tables\": [\n1241\t      {\n1242\t        \"name\": \"Table S5: 7B+ Generator Scale Comparison\",\n1243\t        \"columns\": [\n1244\t          \"Generator\",\n1245\t          \"Params\",\n1246\t          \"In-Dist F1\",\n1247\t          \"LOGO F1\",\n1248\t          \"Drift Rate (nats/depth)\"\n1249\t        ],\n1250\t        \"rows\": [\n1251\t          [\n1252\t            \"Qwen3-0.6B-Base\",\n1253\t            \"0.6B\",\n1254\t            0.8,\n1255\t            0.7,\n1256\t            0.19\n1257\t          ],\n1258\t          [\n1259\t            \"Pythia-1B\",\n1260\t            \"1B\",\n1261\t            0.82,\n1262\t            0.76,\n1263\t            0.17\n1264\t          ],\n1265\t          [\n1266\t            \"OLMo-2-0425-1B\",\n1267\t            \"1.5B\",\n1268\t            0.83,\n1269\t            0.73,\n1270\t            0.16\n1271\t          ],\n1272\t          [\n1273\t            \"Qwen3-1.7B-Base\",\n1274\t            \"1.7B\",\n1275\t            0.82,\n1276\t            0.72,\n1277\t            0.16\n1278\t          ],\n1279\t          [\n1280\t            \"Pythia-2.8B\",\n1281\t            \"2.8B\",\n1282\t            0.84,\n1283\t            0.77,\n1284\t            0.15\n1285\t          ],\n1286\t          [\n1287\t            \"Qwen3-8B-Base\",\n1288\t            \"8B\",\n1289\t            0.78,\n1290\t            0.71,\n1291\t            0.12\n1292\t          ]\n1293\t        ]\n1294\t      },\n1295\t      {\n1296\t        \"name\": \"Table S5b: H1/H2 Verification at 7B+ Scale\",\n1297\t        \"columns\": [\n1298\t          \"Feature\",\n1299\t          \"Depth ρ (8B)\",\n1300\t          \"Depth ρ (≤2.8B mean)\",\n1301\t          \"Classification\"\n1302\t        ],\n1303\t        \"rows\": [\n1304\t          [\n1305\t            \"entropy_mean\",\n1306\t            -0.58,\n1307\t            -0.72,\n1308\t            \"H2 (weaker but confirmed)\"\n1309\t          ],\n1310\t          [\n1311\t            \"ttr\",\n1312\t            -0.39,\n1313\t            -0.48,\n1314\t            \"H2 (weaker but confirmed)\"\n1315\t          ],\n1316\t          [\n1317\t            \"hapax_ratio\",\n1318\t            -0.36,\n1319\t            -0.44,\n1320\t            \"H2 (weaker but confirmed)\"\n1321\t          ],\n1322\t          [\n1323\t            \"entropy_std\",\n1324\t            -0.05,\n1325\t            -0.08,\n1326\t            \"H1 (stationary)\"\n1327\t          ],\n1328\t          [\n1329\t            \"tail_decay\",\n1330\t            0.03,\n1331\t            0.04,\n1332\t            \"H1 (stationary)\"\n1333\t          ]\n1334\t        ]\n1335\t      }\n1336\t    ],\n1337\t    \"figures\": [],\n1338\t    \"analysis\": \"Scale generalization is confirmed with honest attenuation. The key message: dual dynamics is scale-invariant in kind (both H1 and H2 hold) but scale-dependent in magnitude (drift rate compresses with model capacity). This is actually a richer finding than \\\"it works the same at all scales\\\" — it suggests a scaling law for depth signal that could be explored in future work. For the paper, this transforms RC9 from a limitation into a nuanced result.\\n\\n7B+ generator in-distribution F1=0.78 and LOGO F1=0.71 (within [0.65, 0.78] best range) confirm depth signal persists at frontier scale, though weaker than ≤2.8B generators. The signal attenuation is expected: larger models produce more fluent, diverse text that compresses depth-driven distributional drift. Entropy drift per depth step is 0.12 nats vs 0.17 for smaller models (ratio 0.71) — the drift is real but slower. This means deeper chains are needed for equivalent discriminability at larger scale. Crucially, both H2 (entropy_mean ρ=-0.58, monotonic drift) and H1 (shape features near-stationary) hold at 8B scale, confirming dual dynamics generalizes beyond ≤2.8B. The weaker signal is an honest limitation: \\\"depth estimation is harder for larger generators that produce more human-like text.\\\" But the qualitative pattern is identical — this is strong evidence that the phenomenon is fundamental to recursive generation, not a small-model artifact. If anything, the weaker signal at scale makes the finding more interesting: even frontier models cannot fully escape distributional drift under recursion, just compress it.\\n\"\n1339\t  }\n1340\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f50109c-746c-4b8f-986a-349fb461f53e","uuid":"f5d82910-8c2d-4d3d-a1a8-ab69b7d777a9","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:37:05.815Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T221538/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"feature_ablation_and_trend\",\n    \"description\": \"Three-part analysis on MVP data (4609 records, Pythia-2.8B generator, Pythia-410M reference). (A) Feature ablation: reference-only vs surface-only vs full. (B) H1/H2 trend analysis per feature. (C) Perplexity pre-validation for Claim 3.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"full_macro_f1\": 0.8204,\n      \"surface_only_f1\": 0.7628,\n      \"reference_only_f1\": 0.7187,\n      \"full_vs_surface_f1_gap\": 0.058,\n      \"full_vs_reference_f1_gap\": 0.102,\n      \"ref_vs_surf_bootstrap_ci\": \"[0.706,0.733] vs [0.747,0.774]\",\n      \"h2_features\": \"entropy_mean, ttr, hapax_ratio\",\n      \"h1_features\": \"tail_decay, entropy_std, entropy_max, entropy_min\",\n      \"entropy_mean_slope_r2\": 0.91,\n      \"ttr_slope_r2\": 0.88,\n      \"hapax_ratio_slope_r2\": 0.85,\n      \"entropy_std_slope_r2\": 0.12,\n      \"tail_decay_slope_r2\": 0.09,\n      \"median_ppl_by_depth\": [\n        13.5,\n        4.96,\n        3.0,\n        2.07,\n        1.72\n      ],\n      \"ppl_depth_spearman\": -0.97\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: Feature Ablation Results (3-class)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Feature Set\",\n          \"Features\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"gen-0 F1\",\n          \"gen-1 F1\",\n          \"gen-2+ F1\",\n          \"CV std\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Reference-only\",\n            \"entropy_{mean,std,min,max}, tail_decay\",\n            0.72,\n            0.88,\n            0.63,\n            0.65,\n            0.021\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Surface-only\",\n            \"ttr, hapax_ratio\",\n            0.76,\n            0.9,\n            0.69,\n            0.7,\n            0.019\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Full\",\n            \"All 7 features\",\n            0.82,\n            0.95,\n            0.76,\n            0.79,\n            0.013\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: H1/H2 Feature Trend Analysis (linear regression slope per depth)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Feature\",\n          \"Slope\",\n          \"R²\",\n          \"Category\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"entropy_mean\",\n            -0.18,\n            0.91,\n            \"H2-drift\",\n            \"Reference-model surprise decreases monotonically\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ttr\",\n            -0.024,\n            0.88,\n            \"H2-drift\",\n            \"Lexical diversity declines with depth\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"hapax_ratio\",\n            -0.019,\n            0.85,\n            \"H2-drift\",\n            \"Singleton word ratio drops\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"entropy_std\",\n            0.003,\n            0.12,\n            \"H1-stationary\",\n            \"Surprise variance near-constant\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"entropy_max\",\n            0.008,\n            0.15,\n            \"H1-stationary\",\n            \"Tail surprise stable\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"entropy_min\",\n            -0.005,\n            0.11,\n            \"H1-stationary\",\n            \"Floor surprise stable\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"tail_decay\",\n            0.002,\n            0.09,\n            \"H1-stationary\",\n            \"Distributional shape robust\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ablation gap is central to the paper's contribution positioning. The full_vs_surface gap (0.058) and full_vs_reference gap (0.102) confirm that both feature families carry non-redundant signal. Critically, entropy_mean is reference-derived but drifts (H2) — this cross-cuts the surface/reference boundary and is the headline finding. The H1/H2 split is clean: R²≥0.85 for drift features, R²≤0.15 for stationary features. This is the empirical backbone of Claim 2.\\n\\n\\n[ACTUAL — plan-001-fix completed] Full > surface > reference ablation gap confirmed. Surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) are stronger than initially expected (0.76 vs prior dream's 0.63) because vocabulary impoverishment is a robust, high-signal depth cue. Reference features alone achieve 0.72 — weaker than surface alone, overturning the naive expectation that reference features dominate. But their complementary value is clear: full set (0.82) significantly exceeds both subsets. The key insight is that entropy_mean (reference-derived) is an H2/drift feature while entropy_std/max/min (also reference-derived) are H1/stationary — the split is about what's measured (level vs shape), not the feature source. Perplexity monotonically decreases with depth (13.5→1.7) confirming that deeper text is progressively more predictable under the reference model — strong pre-validation for Claim 3's downstream utility hypothesis.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_generator_evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"5 generators: Pythia-2.8B, Pythia-1B, OLMo-2-0425-1B, Qwen3-1.7B-Base, Qwen3-0.6B-Base. 1000 seeds × 5 generators × 5 depths = 25K chains. Leave-one-generator-out (LOGO) CV. Pythia-410M as reference model for all.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"logo_macro_f1_mean\": 0.74,\n      \"logo_macro_f1_std\": 0.03,\n      \"logo_gen0_f1_mean\": 0.87,\n      \"logo_gen1_f1_mean\": 0.68,\n      \"logo_gen2plus_f1_mean\": 0.7,\n      \"in_distribution_pooled_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n      \"pythia_2_8b_held_out_f1\": 0.77,\n      \"pythia_1b_held_out_f1\": 0.76,\n      \"olmo2_held_out_f1\": 0.73,\n      \"qwen3_1_7b_held_out_f1\": 0.72,\n      \"qwen3_0_6b_held_out_f1\": 0.7,\n      \"leave_one_family_out_pythia_f1\": 0.71,\n      \"leave_one_family_out_qwen_f1\": 0.74,\n      \"total_chains\": 23800,\n      \"attrition_rate_pct\": 4.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Leave-One-Generator-Out Cross-Validation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Held-out Generator\",\n          \"Family\",\n          \"Params\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"gen-0 F1\",\n          \"gen-1 F1\",\n          \"gen-2+ F1\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Pythia-2.8B\",\n            \"Pythia\",\n            \"2.8B\",\n            0.77,\n            0.9,\n            0.71,\n            0.72\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Pythia-1B\",\n            \"Pythia\",\n            \"1B\",\n            0.76,\n            0.89,\n            0.7,\n            0.71\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OLMo-2-0425-1B\",\n            \"OLMo\",\n            \"1.5B\",\n            0.73,\n            0.86,\n            0.67,\n            0.68\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-1.7B-Base\",\n            \"Qwen\",\n            \"1.7B\",\n            0.72,\n            0.85,\n            0.66,\n            0.67\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-0.6B-Base\",\n            \"Qwen\",\n            \"0.6B\",\n            0.7,\n            0.83,\n            0.63,\n            0.65\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mean ± std\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.74 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.87 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.68 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.70 ± 0.03\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Per-Generator In-Distribution 5-Fold CV\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Generator\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"CV std\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Pythia-2.8B\",\n            0.84,\n            0.014\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Pythia-1B\",\n            0.82,\n            0.016\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OLMo-2-0425-1B\",\n            0.83,\n            0.015\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-1.7B-Base\",\n            0.82,\n            0.017\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-0.6B-Base\",\n            0.8,\n            0.02\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Pooled (all 5)\",\n            0.83,\n            0.012\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4b: Leave-One-Family-Out\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Held-out Family\",\n          \"Generators Held Out\",\n          \"Macro-F1\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Pythia\",\n            \"Pythia-2.8B + Pythia-1B\",\n            0.71\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen\",\n            \"Qwen3-1.7B + Qwen3-0.6B\",\n            0.74\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OLMo\",\n            \"OLMo-2-0425-1B\",\n            0.73\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"LOGO F1=0.74 is the centerpiece of Claim 1. The ≥0.70 threshold is cleared with margin. Pythia-family slight advantage (0.76-0.77) is a limitation to discuss honestly: Pythia-410M reference model shares tokenizer/architecture with generators. Leave-one-family-out (0.71) still passes the 0.70 threshold, confirming depth signal is generation-process-intrinsic, not family-artifact. The gen-1 vs gen-2+ boundary remains the classification bottleneck across all generators.\\n\\n\\nLOGO mean 0.74 (within [0.70, 0.78] best range) reflects cross-generator generalization grounded in actual plan-001 performance (0.82 in-dist). The ~0.09 drop from in-distribution to LOGO is expected: each generator has its own distributional signature, and training without the held-out generator forces reliance on transfer of the depth-drift pattern. Pythia-family generators transfer best to each other (0.76-0.77) due to shared pretraining distribution (The Pile). Cross-family transfer (Pythia→Qwen, Pythia→OLMo) drops to 0.70-0.73 because the reference model (Pythia-410M) has different baseline expectations for text from non-Pythia generators, adding noise to features. Qwen3-0.6B-Base is hardest (0.70) because its small capacity produces lower-quality text that drifts faster at gen-1, making the depth ordering harder to predict from larger-model-trained classifiers. Leave-one-family-out for Pythia (train on OLMo+Qwen, test on Pythia-2.8B+1B) gives 0.71, confirming the signal is not Pythia-specific. Attrition slightly higher (4.8%) due to Qwen models' less stable generation at depth 4.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_family_reference_model\",\n    \"description\": \"Re-extract features using non-Pythia reference models (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B-Base) on plan_002 data. Key falsification test: does entropy_mean H2 drift persist with non-Pythia reference? If not, dual dynamics is Pythia-specific artifact.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"pythia_410m_ref_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n      \"olmo2_1b_ref_macro_f1\": 0.77,\n      \"qwen3_06b_ref_macro_f1\": 0.74,\n      \"entropy_mean_depth_rho_pythia\": -0.72,\n      \"entropy_mean_depth_rho_olmo\": -0.65,\n      \"entropy_mean_depth_rho_qwen\": -0.58,\n      \"cross_ref_mean_rho\": -0.65,\n      \"entropy_std_depth_rho_pythia\": -0.08,\n      \"entropy_std_depth_rho_olmo\": -0.11,\n      \"entropy_std_depth_rho_qwen\": -0.06,\n      \"feature_correlation_pythia_olmo_entropy_mean\": 0.79,\n      \"feature_correlation_pythia_qwen_entropy_mean\": 0.74,\n      \"h2_drift_confirmed_all_refs\": true,\n      \"h1_stationarity_confirmed_all_refs\": true,\n      \"ensemble_3refs_macro_f1\": 0.85\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Reference Model Comparison (pooled 5-generator data)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Reference Model\",\n          \"Family\",\n          \"Params\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"gen-0 F1\",\n          \"gen-1 F1\",\n          \"gen-2+ F1\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Pythia-410M\",\n            \"Pythia\",\n            \"410M\",\n            0.83,\n            0.94,\n            0.77,\n            0.79\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OLMo-2-1B\",\n            \"OLMo\",\n            \"1B\",\n            0.77,\n            0.89,\n            0.7,\n            0.72\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-0.6B-Base\",\n            \"Qwen\",\n            \"0.6B\",\n            0.74,\n            0.86,\n            0.67,\n            0.69\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Ensemble (3 refs)\",\n            \"Mixed\",\n            \"—\",\n            0.85,\n            0.95,\n            0.79,\n            0.81\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Entropy Feature Depth Correlation (Spearman ρ) by Reference Model\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Feature\",\n          \"Pythia-410M\",\n          \"OLMo-2-1B\",\n          \"Qwen3-0.6B\",\n          \"H1/H2 Classification\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"entropy_mean\",\n            -0.72,\n            -0.65,\n            -0.58,\n            \"H2-drift\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"entropy_std\",\n            -0.08,\n            -0.11,\n            -0.06,\n            \"H1-stationary\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"entropy_max\",\n            0.05,\n            0.03,\n            0.07,\n            \"H1-stationary\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"entropy_min\",\n            -0.1,\n            -0.07,\n            -0.09,\n            \"H1-stationary\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"tail_decay\",\n            0.04,\n            0.06,\n            0.03,\n            \"H1-stationary\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6b: Cross-Reference Feature Correlation (Spearman ρ)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Feature\",\n          \"Pythia↔OLMo\",\n          \"Pythia↔Qwen\",\n          \"OLMo↔Qwen\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"entropy_mean\",\n            0.79,\n            0.74,\n            0.77\n          ],\n          [\n            \"entropy_std\",\n            0.76,\n            0.71,\n            0.73\n          ],\n          [\n            \"tail_decay\",\n            0.68,\n            0.63,\n            0.66\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This experiment eliminates the most dangerous threat to the paper: that dual dynamics is a Pythia artifact. With all three reference models showing consistent H2 drift for entropy_mean (ρ range -0.58 to -0.72) and H1 stationarity for shape features (|ρ|<0.12), the level/shape framework is confirmed as generation-intrinsic. The 3-ref ensemble (0.85) exceeding any single ref confirms that diverse reference perspectives capture complementary drift information. For the paper, Table 6 is the most important evidence for the \\\"what it measures, not where it comes from\\\" argument.\\n\\n\\nentropy_mean ρ with depth ranges from -0.58 to -0.72 across reference models (mean -0.65, within [-0.75, -0.55] best range), confirming H2 drift is generation-intrinsic, not Pythia-specific. All three reference models detect monotonic entropy_mean decrease with depth because any well-trained LM assigns progressively higher probability (lower NLL) to deeper text — the text literally becomes more generic/predictable as generation recurses. Pythia-410M gives strongest signal (-0.72) because it was optimized for this pipeline and its 410M capacity is well-calibrated to detect drift (large enough to model human text, small enough to be \\\"surprised\\\" by generator deviations). OLMo (-0.65) and Qwen (-0.58) are noisier because their different pretraining distributions create different baselines. Crucially, entropy_std remains near-zero correlation with depth across all three models (|ρ|<0.12), confirming shape stationarity (H1) is also reference-model-agnostic. The dual dynamics is a property of the recursive generation process, not the measurement instrument. This is the key falsification test for Claim 2 — it passes.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"downstream_utility\",\n    \"description\": \"Fine-tune OLMo-1B (avoiding Pythia-410M circularity) on depth-stratified subsets. ≥3 random seeds with bootstrap CIs. Compare depth-aware filtering vs FastDetectGPT and LD-Score binary baselines. Sample-count-matched comparison to control data size confound.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"downstream_model\": \"OLMo-1B\",\n      \"n_seeds\": 5,\n      \"ppl_gen0_only_mean\": 31.2,\n      \"ppl_gen0_only_ci\": [\n        30.4,\n        32.0\n      ],\n      \"ppl_gen1_only_mean\": 33.8,\n      \"ppl_gen1_only_ci\": [\n        32.9,\n        34.7\n      ],\n      \"ppl_gen2_only_mean\": 38.5,\n      \"ppl_gen2_only_ci\": [\n        37.2,\n        39.8\n      ],\n      \"ppl_gen3_only_mean\": 43.1,\n      \"ppl_gen3_only_ci\": [\n        41.5,\n        44.7\n      ],\n      \"ppl_gen4_only_mean\": 47.6,\n      \"ppl_gen4_only_ci\": [\n        45.8,\n        49.4\n      ],\n      \"depth_quality_spearman\": 0.74,\n      \"ppl_depth_filtered_gen01_mean\": 32.1,\n      \"ppl_depth_filtered_gen01_ci\": [\n        31.3,\n        32.9\n      ],\n      \"ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt_mean\": 36.8,\n      \"ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt_ci\": [\n        35.6,\n        38.0\n      ],\n      \"ppl_binary_ldscore_mean\": 37.2,\n      \"ppl_binary_ldscore_ci\": [\n        35.9,\n        38.5\n      ],\n      \"ppl_no_filter_mean\": 40.1,\n      \"depth_filter_vs_binary_improvement_pct\": 13.2,\n      \"data_retained_depth_filter_pct\": 44,\n      \"data_retained_binary_pct\": 30,\n      \"ppl_size_matched_depth_filter\": 33.0,\n      \"ppl_size_matched_binary\": 37.0,\n      \"size_matched_ci_non_overlapping\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Downstream Perplexity by Training Data Depth (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Training Data\",\n          \"PPL Mean\",\n          \"95% CI\",\n          \"Samples\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"gen-0 only\",\n            31.2,\n            \"[30.4, 32.0]\",\n            \"~4800\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-1 only\",\n            33.8,\n            \"[32.9, 34.7]\",\n            \"~4700\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-2 only\",\n            38.5,\n            \"[37.2, 39.8]\",\n            \"~4400\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-3 only\",\n            43.1,\n            \"[41.5, 44.7]\",\n            \"~4100\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-4 only\",\n            47.6,\n            \"[45.8, 49.4]\",\n            \"~3800\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: Depth-Aware vs Binary Filtering (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"PPL Mean\",\n          \"95% CI\",\n          \"Data Retained\",\n          \"Δ vs No-Filter\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Depth-filtered (gen-0+1)\",\n            32.1,\n            \"[31.3, 32.9]\",\n            \"44%\",\n            \"-20.0%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FastDetectGPT binary\",\n            36.8,\n            \"[35.6, 38.0]\",\n            \"30%\",\n            \"-8.2%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LD-Score binary\",\n            37.2,\n            \"[35.9, 38.5]\",\n            \"28%\",\n            \"-7.2%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"No filtering\",\n            40.1,\n            \"[38.8, 41.4]\",\n            \"100%\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8b: Size-Matched Comparison (subsample to equal data count)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Matched Count\",\n          \"PPL Mean\",\n          \"95% CI\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Depth-filtered (subsampled)\",\n            \"~5500\",\n            33.0,\n            \"[32.1, 33.9]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Binary-filtered\",\n            \"~5500\",\n            37.0,\n            \"[35.8, 38.2]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CIs non-overlapping\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"YES\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the practical punchline. The 13.2% improvement with CIs non-overlapping under size-matching establishes that depth-aware filtering provides genuine data quality advantage, not just a data quantity effect. gen-1 text is \\\"good enough\\\" for training (PPL 33.8 vs gen-0's 31.2 — 8% gap), but gen-2+ is harmful (PPL 38.5+, >23% worse than gen-0). Binary detectors force an all-or-nothing choice and discard useful gen-1 content. This directly validates Claim 3 under the strengthened protocol (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds, CIs, size-matched).\\n\\n\\nOLMo-1B perplexity increases monotonically with training depth (31.2→47.6) because deeper text has compounded distributional drift from human text — fine-tuning on it shifts OLMo-1B's distribution away from the human test set. The absolute PPL values are higher than the previous dream (which used Pythia-410M) because OLMo-1B is a stronger model with higher baseline expectations. Spearman ρ=0.74 (within [0.65, 0.85]) reflects individual sample variance within depth classes. Depth-aware filtering (keep gen-0+gen-1, PPL=32.1) outperforms binary baselines (~36.8-37.2) by 13.2% (within [8, 20] range) because binary detectors discard all synthetic text — including useful gen-1 content that is distributionally near-human. Binary filters retain only ~30% of data (mostly gen-0) while depth filtering retains 44% (gen-0 + gen-1), providing more and better training data. The size-matched comparison is critical: even when subsampling depth-filtered data to match binary filter count, depth filtering still wins (33.0 vs 37.0, CIs non-overlapping) — confirming the advantage is data quality, not quantity. Using OLMo-1B eliminates the Pythia reference model circularity concern. 5-seed bootstrap CIs are tight enough to establish significance.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"prompt_type_comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare continuation vs elaboration vs inspired_rewriting chains. 1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 5 depths × 3 prompt types.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"continuation_macro_f1\": 0.82,\n      \"elaboration_macro_f1\": 0.77,\n      \"inspired_rewriting_macro_f1\": 0.69,\n      \"continuation_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.17,\n      \"elaboration_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.13,\n      \"inspired_rewriting_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.08,\n      \"cross_prompt_type_macro_f1\": 0.71,\n      \"prompt_drift_rate_f1_spearman\": 0.93\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: Depth Classification by Prompt Type (Pythia-2.8B)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Prompt Type\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"gen-0 F1\",\n          \"gen-1 F1\",\n          \"gen-2+ F1\",\n          \"Drift Rate (nats/depth)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Continuation\",\n            0.82,\n            0.94,\n            0.76,\n            0.78,\n            0.17\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Elaboration\",\n            0.77,\n            0.9,\n            0.7,\n            0.72,\n            0.13\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Inspired rewriting\",\n            0.69,\n            0.84,\n            0.61,\n            0.63,\n            0.08\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cross-prompt LOGO\",\n            0.71,\n            0.85,\n            0.63,\n            0.65,\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ordering (continuation > elaboration > inspired rewriting) aligns with theory: semantic coupling determines drift accumulation rate. This is a supporting result — it enriches the paper but isn't strictly necessary for the core claims. Best used to show that depth signal is graded and mechanism-consistent.\\n\\n\\nContinuation preserves strongest depth signal (0.82) because each step directly extends previous output, maximizing cumulative drift. Elaboration (0.77) injects new semantic direction via prompt instruction, partially resetting drift per step. Inspired rewriting (0.69) provides most semantic freedom — the model can re-anchor to its pretraining distribution at each step, yielding weakest depth accumulation. The strong correlation between per-depth entropy drift rate and F1 (ρ=0.93) confirms the theoretical prediction: more constrained generation → faster drift accumulation → stronger depth signal. Cross-prompt transfer (0.71) is moderate because drift signatures are partially shared but rate differences create distribution shift. This result enriches the theoretical story without changing the core claims.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"paraphrastic_comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Parallel paraphrasing chains (same 1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 5 depths) vs continuation chains. Direct test of attractor convergence (H1) vs monotonic divergence (H2).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"paraphrase_macro_f1_3class\": 0.55,\n      \"paraphrase_gen0_f1\": 0.79,\n      \"paraphrase_gen1_f1\": 0.43,\n      \"paraphrase_gen2plus_f1\": 0.44,\n      \"continuation_macro_f1_3class\": 0.82,\n      \"paraphrase_entropy_drift_depth3\": 0.05,\n      \"continuation_entropy_drift_depth3\": 0.48,\n      \"paraphrase_cycle_detection_pct\": 64,\n      \"ks_statistic_para_vs_cont_depth3\": 0.39,\n      \"ks_p_value\": 1e-05\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: Paraphrastic vs Continuation Chain Dynamics\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Chain Type\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"Entropy Drift @depth3\",\n          \"Cycle Detection %\",\n          \"KS D\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Continuation\",\n            0.82,\n            \"0.48 nats\",\n            \"<5%\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Paraphrasing\",\n            0.55,\n            \"0.05 nats\",\n            \"64%\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"KS test (depth-3)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.39 (p<0.001)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the conceptual anchor connecting our empirical finding to the attractor cycle literature. The clean separation validates why we focus on non-paraphrastic chains and why our approach succeeds where paraphrasing-based analysis would fail at depth estimation beyond ~2 classes.\\n\\n\\nParaphrastic chains show weak depth signal (F1=0.55) because the semantic-preservation constraint creates attractor dynamics — distributional features converge after ~2 steps, consistent with ACL 2025 attractor cycle theory. By depth 3, paraphrastic entropy drift has nearly plateaued (0.05 nats) while continuation drift continues (0.48 nats). The 64% cycle detection rate in paraphrastic chains (depth d closer to d-2 than d-1 in feature space) directly corroborates 2-period attractor cycle predictions. The KS test (D=0.39, p<0.001) provides formal evidence that paraphrastic and continuation depth-3 distributions are distinct. This elevates the paper from \\\"a depth classifier\\\" to \\\"an empirical test of generation dynamics\\\" by connecting to established theoretical literature. Our H2 divergence for non-paraphrastic chains and H1 convergence for paraphrastic chains are two sides of the same coin — the presence or absence of semantic constraint determines whether drift accumulates or converges.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_model_chains\",\n    \"description\": \"Cross-model chains: different generator per depth step (randomly sampled from 5 generators). 1000 seeds × 5 depths. Tests ecological validity — real contamination uses diverse generators.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"cross_model_macro_f1\": 0.71,\n      \"single_model_macro_f1_mean\": 0.82,\n      \"cross_model_gen0_f1\": 0.88,\n      \"cross_model_gen1_f1\": 0.64,\n      \"cross_model_gen2plus_f1\": 0.63,\n      \"cross_vs_single_delta\": -0.11,\n      \"cross_model_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.12\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chain Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Chain Type\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"gen-0 F1\",\n          \"gen-1 F1\",\n          \"gen-2+ F1\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Single-model (mean of 5)\",\n            0.82,\n            0.94,\n            0.76,\n            0.78\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cross-model (random switch)\",\n            0.71,\n            0.88,\n            0.64,\n            0.63\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Δ\",\n            -0.11,\n            -0.06,\n            -0.12,\n            -0.15\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 11-point drop is expected and honestly bounded. For data curation, the key utility is still maintained: the method separates human from synthetic (gen-0 F1=0.88) even under realistic multi-generator contamination. Finer depth estimation within synthetic text is where accuracy degrades — an honest limitation for the Discussion section.\\n\\n\\nCross-model chains lose ~11 points from single-model (0.71 vs 0.82) because generator switching introduces distributional discontinuities at each step — the new generator's idiosyncratic signature partially masks the accumulated depth drift. However, F1=0.71 still far exceeds chance (0.33) because the underlying drift phenomenon persists: regardless of which generator produces each step, cumulative deviation from human text distribution still increases with depth. gen-0 remains well-separated (0.88) — human text is fundamentally distinct from any generator. The gen-1/gen-2+ boundary degrades most (0.64/0.63) because generator-switching noise creates feature-space overlap between these classes. Entropy drift rate (0.12 nats/depth) is lower than single-model (0.17) because each generator transition partially resets the drift trajectory. This is the honest cost of ecological validity — reported transparently, it strengthens the paper.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"multi_domain_seeds\",\n    \"description\": \"4 domains: Wikipedia, news (CNN/DailyMail), scientific (S2ORC), creative (WritingPrompts). 500 seeds per domain × 5 generators × 5 depths.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"wikipedia_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n      \"news_macro_f1\": 0.8,\n      \"scientific_macro_f1\": 0.78,\n      \"creative_macro_f1\": 0.74,\n      \"cross_domain_logo_f1\": 0.74,\n      \"pooled_all_domains_f1\": 0.81,\n      \"domain_f1_variance\": 0.0012\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: Multi-Domain Depth Classification\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Domain\",\n          \"Seeds\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"gen-0 F1\",\n          \"gen-1 F1\",\n          \"gen-2+ F1\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Wikipedia\",\n            500,\n            0.83,\n            0.94,\n            0.77,\n            0.79\n          ],\n          [\n            \"News\",\n            500,\n            0.8,\n            0.92,\n            0.74,\n            0.76\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Scientific\",\n            500,\n            0.78,\n            0.9,\n            0.71,\n            0.73\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Creative\",\n            500,\n            0.74,\n            0.86,\n            0.67,\n            0.69\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Pooled (all)\",\n            2000,\n            0.81,\n            0.92,\n            0.74,\n            0.76\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Leave-one-domain-out\",\n            \"—\",\n            0.74,\n            0.87,\n            0.67,\n            0.69\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Domain generalization confirms distributional drift is not a Wikipedia artifact. The factual-to- creative ordering is interpretable and provides practitioner guidance: depth estimation is most reliable for factual domains. Four domains with LOGO evaluation directly addresses RC6.\\n\\n\\nWikipedia performs best (0.83) as it matches the MVP development setting with structured, information-dense text that generators struggle to faithfully continue. News (0.80) has similar factual density. Scientific text (0.78) has specialized vocabulary that partially masks depth drift in surface features, though reference features still detect it. Creative writing is hardest (0.74) because generators produce more fluent, human-like creative text — the distributional gap between human and generated creative text is smaller, reducing overall drift magnitude. The low F1 variance across domains (0.0012) indicates depth signal is a robust, domain-general phenomenon. Cross-domain LOGO (0.74) confirms generalization without domain-specific calibration. This addresses RC6 (ecological validity) by showing the approach works across diverse text types.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"finer_grained_ordinal\",\n    \"description\": \"5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on plan_002 data. Tests depth resolution ceiling for non-paraphrastic chains.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"ordinal_5class_macro_f1\": 0.58,\n      \"ordinal_gen0_f1\": 0.91,\n      \"ordinal_gen1_f1\": 0.65,\n      \"ordinal_gen2_f1\": 0.48,\n      \"ordinal_gen3_f1\": 0.44,\n      \"ordinal_gen4_f1\": 0.46,\n      \"ordinal_mae\": 0.62,\n      \"ordinal_spearman_rho\": 0.81,\n      \"adjacent_class_confusion_pct\": 74\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 13: 5-Class Ordinal Depth Classification\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Class\",\n          \"Precision\",\n          \"Recall\",\n          \"F1\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"gen-0\",\n            0.92,\n            0.9,\n            0.91\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-1\",\n            0.63,\n            0.67,\n            0.65\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-2\",\n            0.46,\n            0.5,\n            0.48\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-3\",\n            0.43,\n            0.45,\n            0.44\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-4\",\n            0.48,\n            0.44,\n            0.46\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Macro\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            0.58\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 13b: Ordinal Error Statistics\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"Value\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MAE\",\n            0.62\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Spearman ρ (pred vs true)\",\n            0.81\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Adjacent-class confusion\",\n            \"74%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"5-class F1=0.58 characterizes the practical resolution ceiling. The ordinal structure (ρ=0.81) is the more important metric than exact accuracy — for curation, knowing approximate depth is more useful than exact classification. This result differentiates from attractor theory's ~2-class ceiling for paraphrasing.\\n\\n\\n5-class F1=0.58 (within [0.50, 0.65] best range) reflects the resolution ceiling of 7 distributional features. gen-0 remains easy (0.91), gen-1 moderate (0.65), but gen-2/3/4 have heavily overlapping feature distributions (F1=0.44-0.48). Drift accumulates monotonically but with diminishing marginal signal per step — the distributional distance between gen-3 and gen-4 is smaller than between gen-0 and gen-1 because the generation process approaches a distributional attractor. However, the high Spearman ρ (0.81) and the 74% adjacent-class error pattern confirm that the classifier preserves ordinal structure: mistakes are off-by-one, not off-by-three. This supports ordinal regression and directly addresses RC3: non-paraphrastic chains support at least 3 clean classes (vs ~2 for paraphrasing), and the ordinal signal extends to 5 classes even if exact classification weakens. For data curation, coarse buckets (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) are more useful anyway.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"human_edit_robustness\",\n    \"description\": \"Robustness to simulated edits: random word replacement (5/10/20%) and LLM-simulated light editing.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"no_edit_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n      \"random_5pct_f1\": 0.8,\n      \"random_10pct_f1\": 0.75,\n      \"random_20pct_f1\": 0.63,\n      \"llm_light_edit_f1\": 0.73,\n      \"degradation_slope_per_10pct\": -0.1\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 14: Robustness to Simulated Edits\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Edit Type\",\n          \"Edit Rate\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"Δ from Baseline\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"None\",\n            \"0%\",\n            0.83,\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random word replacement\",\n            \"5%\",\n            0.8,\n            \"-0.03\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random word replacement\",\n            \"10%\",\n            0.75,\n            \"-0.08\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random word replacement\",\n            \"20%\",\n            0.63,\n            \"-0.20\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LLM light editing\",\n            \"~8-12%\",\n            0.73,\n            \"-0.10\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Practical takeaway: depth estimation remains usable (F1>0.70) at realistic editing levels (≤10%). Heavy editing (20%+) significantly degrades performance — an honest limitation. The method targets unedited or lightly post-processed synthetic text, which is the majority of web-scraped LLM output.\\n\\n\\n5% random replacement has minimal impact (Δ=-0.03) because uniform token noise preserves relative distributional differences between depth classes. At 10% (0.75, Δ=-0.08), noise begins masking depth-specific features as random words inject unpredictable NLL values. At 20% (0.63, Δ=-0.20), substantial corruption disrupts the fingerprint significantly but stays above chance. LLM light editing (0.73, comparable to 10% random) changes surface statistics but largely preserves distributional structure — edited text occupies similar reference-model feature space because rephrasing doesn't fundamentally change what the text is about. The ~linear degradation (-0.10 F1 per 10% edit rate) provides a practitioner rule-of-thumb for estimating signal reliability at different editing intensities. These results address RC5: the method is viable for lightly edited synthetic text (the common case in web data).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"ld_score_feature\",\n    \"description\": \"Add LD-Score as feature. Pre-registered prediction (RC7): LD-Score is a level feature exhibiting H2 drift.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"full_plus_ld_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n      \"ld_score_only_f1\": 0.55,\n      \"ld_score_permutation_importance\": 0.05,\n      \"ld_score_depth_rho\": -0.52,\n      \"ld_score_h2_confirmed\": true,\n      \"full_plus_ld_vs_full_delta\": 0.01\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S1: LD-Score Feature Integration\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Feature Set\",\n          \"Macro-F1\",\n          \"Δ from Full\",\n          \"LD-Score ρ with Depth\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Full (7 features)\",\n            0.82,\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Full + LD-Score\",\n            0.83,\n            \"+0.01\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LD-Score only\",\n            0.55,\n            \"-0.27\",\n            \"-0.52 (H2 confirmed)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The pre-registered prediction strengthens the paper's credibility. LD-Score drifts as a level feature, exactly as the framework predicts. But its practical value is minimal — the existing 7 features capture the same signal more efficiently. This is a useful negative-ish result that reinforces the sufficiency of the current feature set.\\n\\n\\nLD-Score (character-level distributional signature) exhibits H2 drift (ρ=-0.52) confirming the pre-registered prediction: it is a level feature sensitive to vocabulary/character frequency shifts that accumulate with depth. However, adding it to the full feature set gives only marginal improvement (Δ=+0.01) because its signal is largely redundant with surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) — both measure vocabulary impoverishment at different granularities. LD-Score alone (F1=0.55) is weaker than surface features alone (0.76) because character-level statistics are noisier proxies for depth than word-level statistics. The pre-registered prediction being confirmed is a small but clean validation of the level/shape framework: we predicted LD-Score's behavior class before observing it, and the prediction held. This belongs in supplementary material.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"end_to_end_classifier\",\n    \"description\": \"QLoRA fine-tuned Qwen3-0.6B-Base for end-to-end depth classification. Compares learned representations vs handcrafted distributional features.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"qlora_in_dist_macro_f1\": 0.8,\n      \"qlora_gen0_f1\": 0.93,\n      \"qlora_gen1_f1\": 0.74,\n      \"qlora_gen2plus_f1\": 0.75,\n      \"handcrafted_in_dist_macro_f1\": 0.83,\n      \"qlora_cross_gen_logo_f1\": 0.66,\n      \"handcrafted_cross_gen_logo_f1\": 0.74,\n      \"qlora_training_gpu_hours\": 14,\n      \"handcrafted_training_minutes\": 3\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S2: Feature-Based vs End-to-End Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"In-Dist F1\",\n          \"Cross-Gen LOGO F1\",\n          \"Training Cost\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"XGBoost + handcrafted\",\n            0.83,\n            0.74,\n            \"3 min\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QLoRA Qwen3-0.6B\",\n            0.8,\n            0.66,\n            \"14 GPU·h\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Δ (handcrafted − QLoRA)\",\n            \"+0.03\",\n            \"+0.08\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Handcrafted features outperform, generalize better, and are 4000× cheaper. The cross-gen gap (0.08 vs 0.03 in-dist) is the key diagnostic: QLoRA takes generator-specific shortcuts. This reinforces the contribution positioning — the novelty is the problem formulation and empirical finding, not the classifier architecture.\\n\\n\\nThe QLoRA model achieves competitive in-distribution F1 (0.80) because transformers can implicitly learn distributional features from raw text. However, it underperforms handcrafted features (0.83) for two reasons: (1) handcrafted features distill the reference model's perspective into compact statistics that directly measure drift, while QLoRA must learn this implicitly; (2) XGBoost on 7 features is harder to overfit than a 0.6B model on ~24K examples. The larger cross-gen gap (0.66 vs 0.74) reveals that QLoRA memorizes generator-specific text patterns more than the handcrafted features, which are designed to measure generator-agnostic distributional drift. The 4000× training cost difference (14 GPU-hours vs 3 minutes) makes the feature-based approach clearly preferable. This validates the paper's focus on interpretable features over end-to-end learning and directly answers \\\"why not just fine-tune a model?\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"text_length_control\",\n    \"description\": \"Address length confound (RC10): (A) Length distribution analysis by depth. (B) Length-normalized surface features (TTR, hapax on fixed 200-token windows). (C) Length-matched subsample comparison.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"mean_length_gen0_tokens\": 285,\n      \"mean_length_gen1_tokens\": 268,\n      \"mean_length_gen2_tokens\": 253,\n      \"mean_length_gen3_tokens\": 244,\n      \"mean_length_gen4_tokens\": 238,\n      \"length_depth_spearman\": -0.35,\n      \"length_controlled_full_f1\": 0.8,\n      \"length_controlled_surface_only_f1\": 0.74,\n      \"original_full_f1\": 0.82,\n      \"original_surface_only_f1\": 0.76,\n      \"length_controlled_f1_delta_full\": -0.02,\n      \"length_controlled_f1_delta_surface\": -0.02,\n      \"length_normalized_ttr_depth_rho\": -0.41,\n      \"original_ttr_depth_rho\": -0.48,\n      \"length_matched_full_f1\": 0.81\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S3: Text Length Distribution by Depth\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Depth\",\n          \"Mean Length (tokens)\",\n          \"Std\",\n          \"Median\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"gen-0\",\n            285,\n            82,\n            271\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-1\",\n            268,\n            75,\n            254\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-2\",\n            253,\n            71,\n            240\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-3\",\n            244,\n            68,\n            232\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gen-4\",\n            238,\n            66,\n            226\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S4: Length-Controlled Feature Evaluation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Condition\",\n          \"Full F1\",\n          \"Surface F1\",\n          \"Δ Full\",\n          \"Δ Surface\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Original\",\n            0.82,\n            0.76,\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Length-normalized (200-token window)\",\n            0.8,\n            0.74,\n            \"-0.02\",\n            \"-0.02\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Length-matched subsample\",\n            0.81,\n            0.75,\n            \"-0.01\",\n            \"-0.01\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S4b: Length-Controlled Feature Depth Correlation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Feature\",\n          \"Original ρ\",\n          \"Length-Normalized ρ\",\n          \"Signal Retained\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"ttr\",\n            -0.48,\n            -0.41,\n            \"85%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"hapax_ratio\",\n            -0.44,\n            -0.38,\n            \"86%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"entropy_mean\",\n            -0.72,\n            -0.71,\n            \"99%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Length confound is real but small: ~15% of surface feature signal is length-driven, ~85% is genuine depth signal. Reference features (entropy_mean) are essentially unaffected (99% signal retained) because NLL statistics are length-invariant. The -0.02 F1 delta for both full and surface features confirms the confound doesn't drive the main results. This must be in the paper to preempt RC10 and maintain credibility.\\n\\n\\nText length decreases modestly with depth (285→238 tokens, ρ=-0.35) because generators tend to produce slightly shorter continuations at deeper steps due to vocabulary impoverishment and repetition. TTR and hapax_ratio are mechanically length-dependent (shorter texts have higher TTR), which could inflate surface feature depth signal. However, length-controlled analysis shows minimal F1 drop: full features decline only 0.02 (0.82→0.80, within [-0.03, 0.0] best range) and surface features also drop only 0.02 (0.76→0.74). This confirms that surface features are tracking genuine vocabulary impoverishment, not length artifacts. Length-normalized TTR still shows significant depth correlation (ρ=-0.41 vs original -0.48), meaning ~85% of the signal survives length control. Length-matched subsampling (equalizing length distributions across depths) yields F1=0.81, confirming the classifier is not exploiting length as a shortcut. This is a critical confound control that must appear in the main paper — reviewers will look for it.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"large_scale_generator\",\n    \"description\": \"Add Qwen3-8B-Base (or similar 7B+ model) to test scale generalization. 1000 seeds × 5 depths on RTX 5090 (32GB). Addresses RC9.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"qwen3_8b_in_dist_macro_f1\": 0.78,\n      \"qwen3_8b_gen0_f1\": 0.91,\n      \"qwen3_8b_gen1_f1\": 0.71,\n      \"qwen3_8b_gen2plus_f1\": 0.73,\n      \"qwen3_8b_logo_f1\": 0.71,\n      \"qwen3_8b_entropy_mean_depth_rho\": -0.58,\n      \"qwen3_8b_ttr_depth_rho\": -0.39,\n      \"qwen3_8b_entropy_drift_per_depth\": 0.12,\n      \"small_generators_mean_drift_per_depth\": 0.17,\n      \"drift_ratio_7b_vs_small\": 0.71,\n      \"h2_confirmed_7b\": true,\n      \"h1_confirmed_7b\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S5: 7B+ Generator Scale Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Generator\",\n          \"Params\",\n          \"In-Dist F1\",\n          \"LOGO F1\",\n          \"Drift Rate (nats/depth)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-0.6B-Base\",\n            \"0.6B\",\n            0.8,\n            0.7,\n            0.19\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Pythia-1B\",\n            \"1B\",\n            0.82,\n            0.76,\n            0.17\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OLMo-2-0425-1B\",\n            \"1.5B\",\n            0.83,\n            0.73,\n            0.16\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-1.7B-Base\",\n            \"1.7B\",\n            0.82,\n            0.72,\n            0.16\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Pythia-2.8B\",\n            \"2.8B\",\n            0.84,\n            0.77,\n            0.15\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-8B-Base\",\n            \"8B\",\n            0.78,\n            0.71,\n            0.12\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S5b: H1/H2 Verification at 7B+ Scale\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Feature\",\n          \"Depth ρ (8B)\",\n          \"Depth ρ (≤2.8B mean)\",\n          \"Classification\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"entropy_mean\",\n            -0.58,\n            -0.72,\n            \"H2 (weaker but confirmed)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ttr\",\n            -0.39,\n            -0.48,\n            \"H2 (weaker but confirmed)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"hapax_ratio\",\n            -0.36,\n            -0.44,\n            \"H2 (weaker but confirmed)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"entropy_std\",\n            -0.05,\n            -0.08,\n            \"H1 (stationary)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"tail_decay\",\n            0.03,\n            0.04,\n            \"H1 (stationary)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Scale generalization is confirmed with honest attenuation. The key message: dual dynamics is scale-invariant in kind (both H1 and H2 hold) but scale-dependent in magnitude (drift rate compresses with model capacity). This is actually a richer finding than \\\"it works the same at all scales\\\" — it suggests a scaling law for depth signal that could be explored in future work. For the paper, this transforms RC9 from a limitation into a nuanced result.\\n\\n7B+ generator in-distribution F1=0.78 and LOGO F1=0.71 (within [0.65, 0.78] best range) confirm depth signal persists at frontier scale, though weaker than ≤2.8B generators. The signal attenuation is expected: larger models produce more fluent, diverse text that compresses depth-driven distributional drift. Entropy drift per depth step is 0.12 nats vs 0.17 for smaller models (ratio 0.71) — the drift is real but slower. This means deeper chains are needed for equivalent discriminability at larger scale. Crucially, both H2 (entropy_mean ρ=-0.58, monotonic drift) and H1 (shape features near-stationary) hold at 8B scale, confirming dual dynamics generalizes beyond ≤2.8B. The weaker signal is an honest limitation: \\\"depth estimation is harder for larger generators that produce more human-like text.\\\" But the qualitative pattern is identical — this is strong evidence that the phenomenon is fundamental to recursive generation, not a small-model artifact. If anything, the weaker signal at scale makes the finding more interesting: even frontier models cannot fully escape distributional drift under recursion, just compress it.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1340,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1340}}}
